    {
      "version": "0.17.1",
      "released": "2026-07-31",
      "headline": "The local dashboard now opens complete canonical artifact records instead of summary-only placeholders",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01701-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Work cards mount their real bodies, envelopes, events and references",
          "detail": "The local HTML dashboard now resolves every visible incoming and outgoing row through the same canonical cache projection as `a2a show`. Selecting a work card displays its original Markdown body, schema-owned envelope facts, lifecycle events, references, digest and sync state instead of reporting that no detail record exists. Space-qualified lookups also keep identical artifact IDs in different spaces distinct and deterministic.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "artifact details",
            "multi-space dashboards"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update when 0.17.1 is released to inspect complete artifact details in generated dashboards; no space or document migration is required.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a version"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01701-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Passive dashboard snapshots retain canonical attention and move ownership",
          "detail": "Dashboard-only inbox and outbox snapshots now carry the existing canonical action reasons and whose-move projection without advancing the CLI inbox cursor. Approval gates, disputes and other real attention signals therefore reach severity badges and overview counts, while routine agent work remains outside the narrower human-attention queue. Existing inbox, outbox and show JSON contracts remain unchanged.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "attention badges",
            "overview"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.18.0",
      "released": "2026-08-01",
      "headline": "A document waiting on you hands its move back to your agent as a copyable prompt",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-1",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The dashboard tells you when there is nothing to see, and a thread that is finished says so",
          "detail": "Every list and detail pane that can legitimately be empty now renders one shared empty state instead of a hollow card, a blank panel or an unrelated record borrowed from elsewhere. Overview's detail pane belongs to its own queue, so \"nothing is waiting on you\" no longer sits beside a fully rendered document. Thread closure is derived rather than stored: an open item whose only remaining affordance is the owner's own escape hatch owes nobody a move, so a settled thread reads as settled instead of claiming to wait on others forever. Integrity stops being a destination and becomes a qualifier at the bottom of Spaces, hidden entirely when there is nothing to qualify; the nav slot it frees goes to Docs, which gives the embedded documentation corpus the same reading layout the website publishes.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "threads",
            "overview",
            "spaces",
            "docs"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-2",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Versions carries the full changelog, documents open in full, and the Russian interface reads like Russian",
          "detail": "The Versions view now projects the complete authored release notes from the same source the website publishes, with the update-candidate card hidden when no candidate exists. Any artifact can be opened as it exists on disk — the whole frontmatter as a table plus the verbatim body. On the exchange map, Escape and a backdrop click close full width, edge and system popovers state their facts in a table rather than a paragraph, and the legend holds its layout in both locales. Cyrillic font subsets are embedded, so the Russian interface no longer falls back to a system font, and short in-place hints explain the protocol vocabulary the screens use.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "versions",
            "exchange map",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-3",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Dashboard cards, the causal timeline and the Versions surface are legible at a glance",
          "detail": "Each lifecycle transition on a thread's timeline now carries its own colour, document markers are larger than the events between them, and clicking a relation focuses the document it names while everything else dims. Compact cards show the lifecycle state they were previously silent about, every type badge survives both card backgrounds, and compact and expanded cards share one header grammar. Work gains a filter by document type; Contracts names provider and consumers outright and reads in one column. Versions now projects the same surface the published changelog does — kind and action filters, copyable detect/run commands, the version index and a release timeline. Ages and dates are humanised and localised in one place, so a raw Go duration no longer reaches a reader.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "threads",
            "work",
            "contracts",
            "versions"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-4",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "The dashboard names each page, speaks the lifecycle in words, and says how current it is",
          "detail": "Every page heading now carries an icon, Overview is titled Overview and states in its description what it used to state in its headline, and its counters span the width. Lifecycle transitions reach a Russian reader as Russian verbs rather than protocol enums, and the state an author claimed is shown as a pill from the one status vocabulary instead of an English word in the middle of a sentence. Focusing a document in a thread is released by a click on any empty space. A scope with nothing in it shows one empty state across the full width instead of two saying the same thing. Snapshot freshness moved out of the Overview column into a Space health dialog that states the writer floor as the comparison it is — what the space requires against the binary you run.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "overview",
            "threads",
            "contracts",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "An install with no space connected no longer reads as healthy",
          "detail": "The dashboard's space-health indicator treated green as its default, so a setup with no space connected at all showed the same signal as one where every space is current — over a dialog with nothing in it. An unconnected scope now takes a neutral indicator and states why it is empty: there is nowhere to read from, which is not the same as having nothing to report.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "overview"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-6",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "One header line, one scope switcher, one empty state, and Work becomes Exchange",
          "detail": "The dashboard header listed every connected space as a chip on a second row that grew with the number of spaces; scope is now one control that stands beside the page it scopes, on the six views where changing it changes what is shown, with space freshness at the foot of its own list. The Work view is named Exchange, since incoming and outgoing documents in flight are an exchange and not a to-do list, and its filters are one segmented control (all, incoming, outgoing) plus a type menu that names the count behind each type instead of a chip row that wrapped at every width; both dropdowns close on Escape or a click outside them. The empty state is one component with two container-decided sizes rather than four picked per call site, its spark now travels back and forth, and a screen with nothing on it shows exactly one of them. Page headings are a single block for all nine views, so the air above and below a title is the same everywhere. Event lists dropped the event ULIDs and artifact digests a reader cannot act on, and the three facts a static read deliberately does not check now say so in the reader's own language.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "header",
            "exchange",
            "empty states",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-7",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "A thread now says whose move it is, by name",
          "detail": "The thread panel was headed \"Whose move\" over a sentence explaining how the answer is derived, leaving the reader to work the answer out from the list below — the one thing the heading existed to save them. It now states it: waiting on you, on a named system, or on both, with the count of documents behind it. Page descriptions say what is on the page rather than how the protocol underneath it behaves, and name every control the page actually has. The thread list names the system too, instead of \"waiting on others\". Underneath that sat a second defect: the demo fixture predates the field that separates \"someone still owes a move\" from \"not yet terminal\", so decoding it marked every open item as owing nothing and the panel rendered with an empty list. Both that field and the new list of who is owed are now derived from the facts the fixture states rather than trusted to be restated in it. Page descriptions were rewritten in each language on its own terms.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "threads",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-8",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Filters that answer a real question, badges with one casing rule, and prose without dashes",
          "detail": "Exchange gains a deadline toggle, because \"what has a clock on it\" is a question with one answer, and its rows now show the deadline itself rather than how long ago the document last moved. Contracts gains the two filters a contract list is actually opened with: whose it is, since publishing one and depending on one are different jobs, and which consumer lines have fallen off the provider's latest major. The type menu shows each type as its own badge instead of teaching a second, colourless vocabulary for the same eight things. Every badge is lowercase now, with uppercase reserved for identifiers the protocol owns. A document card lists its events newest first, the way a thread does. The version panel names who still sits on each version, which is what decides whether it can ever be switched off, states the sunset and the successor as sentences on their own lines, and makes the deprecation announcement open as the document it is. Em dashes are gone from the dashboard's own prose.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "exchange",
            "contracts",
            "badges",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01800-9",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A document waiting on you hands its move back to your agent as a copyable prompt",
          "detail": "Full autonomy is the goal, and until it arrives a person sometimes sees an arriving document before their agent does. Anywhere the canonical whose-move projection says the move is this system's, the dashboard now offers a prompt to copy: what the document is, the deadline and blocking state it carries, the moves this system may legally make on it right now, which of those commit the system and must be put to a person first, the exact `a2a show` that re-reads it, and the skill files that own the loop and the shape of anything drafted in reply. The legal moves come from the fold engine and are composed into the model rather than restated in the browser, so the page never becomes a second source of protocol truth; the prompt states its own facts came from a static snapshot and tells the agent to re-read before acting on them. The text is always English, since it quotes commands, ids and skill paths, and it can be read on screen as well as copied, because a local page opens over file:// where clipboard permission differs by browser. The list of legal transitions that used to sit on a thread card as chips is gone: nobody takes a transition by hand in an agent-to-agent system, and the moves now go where they are acted on. Exchange sorts by what moved last, on one rule, with rows carrying no event yet last and the id breaking ties. Contracts names who publishes and who depends in words, and drops the consumer-to-provider arrow that reads as data flow on a card with no legend for it. Every control in a filter row is one height. A thread hands over once, not once per document. Three primary buttons in a row announce three main actions, which means none, and a conversation is one job anyway: the thread's prompt carries every document in it that is this system's to move on, plus what the other side still owes as context, so an agent acts from the whole situation instead of three times from three copies of the same instructions. The documents under it are a list of facts. Sending a reader to Exchange instead was not an option: the open items a thread folds to are a strictly larger set than the exchange rows, and a requirement this system published or a response it already gave appears in neither inbox nor outbox. \"Blocking\" is gone as a word, because it never said who was blocking whom and the two readings are opposites. `blocking: true` means the AUTHOR has stopped and cannot continue until the document is answered, so every label now names the party who has to move for the work to resume: the recipient. An incoming one reads \"blocked on you\", an outgoing one names the system being waited on, and the detail pane's badge carries a hint that says who stopped, who can start them again, by when and at what priority. The exchange map says how many are stopped on a lane and names the party in the lane's own popover. An open row in a master/detail list is now simply the one at full strength, with the rest stepped back and hover bringing them forward; the ring it used to wear sat on top of a type badge, a state pill, a bold title and a participant line and competed with all four. The explanation hangs off the badge itself and opens on hover or on a tap, rather than off a separate mark beside it: a reader who wonders what two words mean looks at those two words.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "exchange",
            "threads",
            "contracts",
            "agent handover"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.18.1",
      "released": "2026-08-01",
      "headline": "Artifact cards become a readable document surface, with agent-speed deadlines and complete threads",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01801-1",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Artifact cards render safe Markdown, disclose metadata once, and expand into a full reading surface",
          "detail": "The Exchange detail card now renders server-side GFM with headings, lists, tables, blockquotes and code instead of printing Markdown syntax as an oversized text block. Its compact view shows roughly five lines behind a fade and disclosure that closes when the reader clicks outside; full width shows the complete document and drops the misleading \"document preview\" label. The old second document modal and its duplicate button are removed. Five high-value frontmatter fields sit below the deadline/expectation callout, every remaining field stays available in a styled disclosure, and the duplicate Facts section is gone. Open thread and expand controls share the top-right action area, the artifact ID links to the exact GitHub revision and canonical path, and Event history has more space around each entry. The same surface stays within 390 CSS pixels: the header collapses to the active view plus More and every master/detail grid can shrink to the viewport.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "exchange",
            "artifact detail",
            "mobile layout",
            "ru locale"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01801-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A one-document conversation is now a thread instead of disappearing from Threads",
          "detail": "Threads no longer discard a valid conversation merely because it has one artifact and no reply yet. A freshly submitted request is therefore visible immediately with its opener, participants, open item and computed next move; the two Axon requests that prompted this fix now appear as two independent threads rather than only as Exchange rows.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "threads"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01801-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Doctor and mirror walks stop reporting canonical space infrastructure as broken artifacts",
          "detail": "Root branch-protection guidance and files under the space's .github directory are infrastructure, not exchange artifacts. Mirror walks now classify them through the canonical space-path rule before attempting frontmatter decoding, so doctor no longer raises misleading unreadable artifact findings for those files while a participant-owned README still reports honestly when it looks like a malformed artifact.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor",
            "mirror",
            "space health"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01801-4",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Agents choose one- or two-day deadlines and correct sent commitments without rewriting history",
          "detail": "A response-bearing cross-system ask now carries an agent-chosen date at the speed of an AI-to-AI exchange: created date plus one day when blocking or p1, plus two days otherwise. A longer window is valid only when the body names an external non-agent constraint; routine deadlines are not sent to a human for scheduling. The operating loop now also makes corrections explicit. Explanation-only changes use an append-only note; changing the deadline, acceptance criteria, requested result, addressee or meaning requires a complete successor on the same thread followed by supersede. No correction type and no in-place edit are introduced.\n",
          "affects": [
            "agent skill",
            "work requests",
            "requirements",
            "deadlines",
            "threads"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Reinstall or update the embedded a2ahub skill so authoring agents use the new deadline and correction rules.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.18.2",
      "released": "2026-08-01",
      "headline": "Notes land cleanly, release versions stay current, and the public product surface matches the dashboard",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "A failed Git push is no longer mistaken for denied access just because its path contains 403",
          "detail": "The fork fallback now recognizes GitHub's complete HTTP 403 failure phrase instead of matching the three digits anywhere in stderr. A local path or generated fixture name containing 403 therefore remains the original push error and cannot trigger an unrelated fork attempt.\n",
          "affects": [
            "Git push classification",
            "fork fallback"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "The classifier correction is automatic and needs no configuration.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A note is validated before its pull request opens and remains legal in every artifact state",
          "detail": "`a2a note` and its MCP twin now ask the shared fold legality engine before writing. A note remains transition-free and does not change the artifact state, but only either party named by the artifact may add one. This is the same rule the space validator applies, so an authorized note on a submitted response passes the required check instead of opening a pull request that can never merge, while an outsider is refused locally before any branch or pull request is created.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a note",
            "MCP lifecycle tools",
            "fold legality",
            "space validation"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update the a2a binary before adding notes to active exchange artifacts; no document or space migration is required.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a version"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The website and local release tooling no longer report the release before the one actually published",
          "detail": "Public releases are now derived only from exact immutable tag refs, and the Pages build fetches complete tag history. After a proven candidate is promoted, the publisher imports the exact verified public tag back into the private source checkout before it returns. This closes the filtered history gap that left the website, roadmap and release-aware agents on 0.18.0 even though GitHub, the installed binary and operated spaces were already on 0.18.1. Authored notes without a matching tag remain hidden as release candidates rather than being mistaken for shipped software.\n",
          "affects": [
            "release publisher",
            "changelog",
            "roadmap",
            "GitHub Pages"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "The corrected release pipeline keeps future cuts current automatically.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-3",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The public site demonstrates the real dashboard instead of describing it with a second interface",
          "detail": "The product homepage now embeds the same exchange map, artifact proof, status badges, filters, disclosures and visual tokens as the local dashboard. Features is generated from the dashboard Guide source, public page headers follow one component grammar, and compact cards, popovers, modals, mobile layouts and dark mode share the same interaction and spacing rules. Human owners can carry locally cached GitHub avatars after an explicit sync while every offline render keeps a monogram fallback and performs no network request. Doctor reports missing active-owner images as a non-blocking advisory with the exact `a2a sync` repair, so people never need to inspect or manage the cache themselves.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2ahub.dev",
            "a2a html",
            "a2a doctor",
            "design tokens",
            "dashboard map",
            "avatar cache"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required; a normal `a2a sync` refreshes disposable participant avatars when network access is available.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-4",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Search engines and agents get canonical, complete and provenance-labelled product evidence",
          "detail": "The static site now publishes canonical route metadata, a sitemap, Atom release feed, permissive crawler policy, structured data, security.txt, a compact llms.txt and a complete llms-full.txt assembled from the current embedded documentation and tagged releases. The synthetic dashboard is excluded from indexing, Google Analytics loads only on the public site, and the quality gate checks accessibility, links, metadata, bundle shape and Lighthouse budgets before Pages deploys. The shared runtime also keeps unbooted design markup out of the first visible frame, eliminating a timing-dependent changelog layout shift; a deliberately delayed-runtime browser check now guards that first-paint contract.\n",
          "affects": [
            "SEO",
            "agent discovery",
            "llms.txt",
            "accessibility",
            "performance",
            "analytics"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No configuration or data migration is required.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01802-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Prompt refreshes finish independently without turning optional avatar downloads into shell latency",
          "detail": "The detached statusline refresh now owns a real operating-system session, carries a five-second end-to-end budget and skips optional avatar downloads while retaining mirror and update-notice refreshes. Explicit `a2a sync` still refreshes avatars normally. Scoped e2e verification also refuses a cached CLI from an earlier run, so a stale binary can no longer hide a regression in the source currently under test.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a statusline",
            "background sync",
            "avatar cache",
            "scoped verification"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "The prompt refresh and verification safeguards apply automatically.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.0",
      "released": "2026-08-05",
      "headline": "A2A shows the work behind an exchange and makes every contract version reproducible",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-0",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "The dashboard distinguishes completed exchanges from work that is still happening",
          "detail": "Agents can record durable work checkpoints and keep a short machine-local lease while they are active. Overview folds those facts into a separate operational timeline without changing the attention queue: one process can show several simultaneous actors, what each one is doing, who or what they are waiting for, and whether the last evidence is current, stale or unknown. Static HTML and the loopback server consume the same canonical snapshot, so a closed thread is no longer misreported as proof that all implementation work stopped. Missing reports remain visibly unknown instead of being rounded down to inactivity.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a work",
            "a2a html",
            "a2a dashboard",
            "a2a serve",
            "Overview",
            "MCP"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update the binary and installed skill before agents begin reporting work; existing exchange history remains readable and needs no rewrite.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a version"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-1",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A contract version can be preflighted, published, recovered, materialized and checked from one immutable plan",
          "detail": "Contract publication now freezes one bounded candidate, finalizes its descriptor before hashing, records the exact carried set and digest profile, and uses the same plan for preflight and publish. Historical resolution identifies the unique version-establishing commit, including retired versions; materialize reproduces those verified bytes without overwriting divergent local work; check runs the declared self-suite or a payload against that exact version offline. CLI and MCP share the same services, multi-space requests fail closed when ambiguous, and interrupted writes resume the same operation instead of silently creating a new version or pull request. Fresh JSON-Schema contract authoring now creates one complete declared-v2 staging candidate — descriptor, schema and fixtures — so its first preflight and publication use the same real agent workflow instead of requiring a hand-assembled descriptor.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract new",
            "a2a contract preflight",
            "a2a contract publish",
            "a2a contract materialize",
            "a2a contract check",
            "a2a_contract",
            "contract/v2",
            "event/v2"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update the binary before publishing with the v2 carried-set profile. Existing v1 history remains readable and does not need migration.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a version"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "Partial GitHub writes preserve enough evidence to repair the same operation safely",
          "detail": "Every post-push outcome now retains the remote stage, branch, tree and pull request reference that actually exist. Retry first proves that evidence and repairs the same head or pull request; it does not report a generic failure that tempts an agent to submit a duplicate. Publication recovery also returns the exact recomputed plan, and the caller-observed space writer floor is required and revalidated after refresh instead of trusting a missing or stale authoring decision.\n",
          "affects": [
            "shared write funnel",
            "GitHub recovery",
            "contract publish",
            "CLI",
            "MCP"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Recovery and result-shape corrections apply automatically to new writes.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Producer claims, protocol status and current work no longer collapse into one misleading state",
          "detail": "Applicable first-party events carry evaluator-owned receipt and provenance evidence, and mismatches remain diagnostic facts rather than a second editable state. The operational projection ignores lifecycle escape hatches that owe nobody a move, synchronizes cache refresh with concurrent server rendering, and keeps protocol completion separate from reported implementation activity. CLI, MCP, static HTML and server reads therefore agree on what was committed and on what is merely unknown locally. Work-checkpoint validation now treats the absent producer stamp as pending only inside the funnel's internal V2 candidate phase, then revalidates the stamped final bytes in full before any write; malformed, caller-supplied or final missing stamps still fail closed.\n",
          "affects": [
            "event validation",
            "receipt evidence",
            "operational projection",
            "cache refresh",
            "SSE"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "The corrected folds and projections are rebuilt from existing Git history.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-6",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A local server whose refresh is failing no longer looks healthy",
          "detail": "`a2a serve` keeps answering from the last good snapshot when its background refresh fails, which is the right thing to do — but it used to do it silently, so a reader could not tell live data from data that stopped updating an hour ago. Every affected response now carries an `X-A2A-Stale` header naming the reason, and the terminal running the server gets one line when the refresh starts failing and one when it recovers, never one per refresh. The snapshot document itself is unchanged: what is stale is the server's own copy, not a fact about the spaces it projects.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a serve",
            "local dashboard",
            "operational snapshot"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A healthy server behaves and responds exactly as before.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Publishing a breaking change under a compatible version number is refused where you typed it",
          "detail": "`a2a contract publish` now refuses, before it writes anything, when the publication it planned contradicts itself — a minor or patch version declared over content that breaks the existing fixtures. The refusal names the policy code, the exact file and the reason, so the next move is readable from the failed command. Previously the command exited successfully and opened a pull request, and the contradiction only surfaced as a red merge gate minutes later; the merge gate still runs the same checks, so nothing incompatible ever reached the shared branch, but an agent had already moved on by then and left a branch and a pull request behind. The refusal keys on the publication's own violations, not on its review gate, so a first publish and an honest major bump are unaffected, and recovery of an already-published contract is never blocked by a finding its own publication carries. A refused publication plan now also names each issue's rule, file and detail instead of reporting how many issues there were, so a refusal can be acted on from the message alone.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish",
            "contract compatibility",
            "agent feedback loop"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A publication that was already correct behaves identically; one that contradicted its own version number now fails at the command instead of at the merge gate.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-8",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A contract's first publication is resolvable again",
          "detail": "A contract drafted from the shipped template carried `version: 1.0.0` before it was published anything. Publishing 1.0.0 then wrote a descriptor identical to the one already on the branch, so the publication's own commit carried nothing but its event — and a version is resolvable only from the commit where the descriptor's version changes. The publication succeeded, opened a pull request and merged, and the contract was unresolvable from that moment: materializing that version failed, and so did every later version, which has to resolve the first one as its compatibility baseline. Nothing said so at the time. A drafted contract now starts at `0.0.0`, which is what an unpublished contract has always meant everywhere else in the product, and publishing sets the real version. Where a descriptor was already authored at the version being published, `a2a contract publish` refuses before it writes, naming the contract, the version and what to change, instead of landing a publication that can never be read back. That refusal is what covers a contract drafted before this release, and a contract rendered from the frozen v1 template, which still carries 1.0.0 and cannot be changed without breaking the reproducibility of everything already published under it. The template's note on that field states who owns it rather than what state it is in, because publishing rewrites the value and leaves the comment: anything phrased as \"unpublished\" would have shipped inside every published descriptor, contradicting the version beside it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish",
            "a2a contract materialize",
            "a2a new",
            "contract templates"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Contracts drafted with an earlier binary still read `version: 1.0.0`. Publishing one is now refused rather than silently broken: set the descriptor's version to 0.0.0 in a follow-up submit, then publish. Contracts already published correctly are unaffected.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a contract preflight \u003cXC-id\u003e --version 1.0.0"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-9",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A maintenance release on an older version line is possible again",
          "detail": "Publishing a maintenance version while a newer major was live always failed. The publication computed which files it was replacing, and the exact bytes it expected to find, from the version it was checking compatibility against — not from the version whose files are actually on the branch. With 1.0.0, 1.1.0 and 2.0.0 published in that order, a 1.2.0 release prepared from the 1.1.0 baseline described a tree that was not there, and the write was refused every time with a mismatched precondition on the contract descriptor. The two are now separate: compatibility is still checked against the highest published version below the one being released, while the write is described against whatever the most recent publication left on the branch. Publishing on the newest line is unaffected — there the two answers were always the same, which is why nothing caught this until a contract with three live lines was exercised end to end. That path is now covered against a real provider: a contract carrying 1.0.0, 1.1.0, 2.0.0 and a maintenance 1.2.0 at once, deprecated and retired mid-window, with a consumer that registered after the deprecation was announced.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish",
            "contract versioning"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A publication that already worked behaves identically. A maintenance release that could not be made before now succeeds.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Stale-binary and repository-health diagnostics now point to the exact repair",
          "detail": "A write refused by the space floor names both the running and required versions and tells the agent to update a2a. Repository health no longer mistakes the checkout README for a malformed exchange artifact, while cleanup retries confirm that an already-removed remote branch is truly absent before reporting success. The release live harness follows the same canonical work result, latest-report projection and full materialize-edit-publish contract flow as real agents, so candidate evidence exercises the shipped flows rather than a test-only approximation. Its local-server proof also runs the real bounded sync cadence and filters SSE keepalives from semantic revision events; it now bounds only the read rather than the whole event request, so the pull requests it lands mid-scenario can no longer expire the stream and discard the buffered revision it exists to observe, and its conformance proof accepts the whole-document JSON pointer that a root-level schema violation legitimately carries. Every live run now retains its rendered report next to the evidence it produced, so a verdict is readable after the session that produced it ends rather than only in that session's terminal. The harness also shuts its local server down at the row that started it, restores a repository setting it borrowed on every exit path, and reads a write-free proof as \"nothing appeared\" rather than \"the set is identical\", so provider housekeeping can no longer be reported as a write the release did not make.\n",
          "affects": [
            "write preflight",
            "a2a doctor",
            "cache visibility",
            "GitHub cleanup",
            "release live E2E"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "The corrected diagnostics and repository classification apply automatically after updating the binary.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01900-7",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "Data can be packed against an exact contract version, delivered, fetched and judged, and a failed attempt superseded",
          "detail": "Two agent-run projects can now complete the whole request, work, delivery, verdict and retry cycle over one private exchange space, with every verdict bound to the exact bytes it judged. A producer packs a result against a pinned contract version offline and learns it is wrong before anything reaches the shared space: an entry that does not match the contract's schema refuses the whole pack and names the file, and the record inside it when the payload is line-delimited. Delivery lands the payload, its manifest and the handoff that carries them in a single commit through the existing write funnel, so nothing about the exchange lifecycle changes and no new document type appears. A consumer fetches with every entry's digest re-proven before a single byte is written, re-runs the contract's own conformance checks against what actually arrived, and writes a structured report whose verdict is derived from its checks rather than authored. A failed attempt is superseded by a new immutable one, and what failed and why stays readable. Deliveries and their verdicts appear in the thread under the handoff that carries them, with an unverified delivery visibly distinct from a passed one. Recording a verdict is its own step: verifying judges and prints, and asking it to record writes the report and the lifecycle transition together, in the direction the report itself derived — there is no flag that lets a caller declare a pass over a failing report. The loop was proven end to end between two parties before release, and that proof is what found and closed the defects that would otherwise have reached the first real exchange: verification could not resolve the contract of any package packing had produced, delivery refused every real manifest, and a contract published under the older digest profile reported having no schemas at all. Packing now defaults an attempt's lifetime to a week and refuses a non-positive one, so an attempt is never minted already expired; it prints the staged path and the exact next command rather than leaving it to be reconstructed; and delivery refuses a supersession flag that belongs to packing instead of accepting it and doing nothing with it. Deliveries and their verdicts reach the dashboard through the same projection the rest of a thread is built from, with a delivery nobody has judged yet rendered as exactly that rather than as one that passed. The thread names the group as data, names each deliverable rather than labelling it only by an opaque package id, and gives every failing check its own row — the file, the record it is aimed at, and the rule in words — so a reader can see what to fix without decoding a line of identifiers. A package the local mirror cannot resolve says so as its own state, in the colour of something unseen rather than something failed. The loop is reachable from both surfaces. The MCP tool was specified, catalogued and schema-complete while having no backend at all — every call refused as unconfigured — so an agent on a tool-calling harness could not run the loop the documentation described. It now runs the same production core the CLI does, selects a space explicitly and refuses an ambiguous one rather than choosing. Its verify input is the same record switch the CLI has, never a caller-supplied verdict: the earlier schema would have let a tool call declare a pass, which is the one thing this loop exists to make unrepresentable. Packing through either surface now applies the same one-week expiry floor, because that default lived in the CLI's own flag layer and a tool call omitting it would have minted a package expired at birth.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a data pack",
            "a2a data deliver",
            "a2a data fetch",
            "a2a data verify",
            "a2a_data",
            "data-package/v1",
            "verification-report/v1",
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update the binary before packing or verifying a delivery. Existing exchange history is unaffected and needs no migration.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a version"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.1",
      "released": "2026-08-05",
      "headline": "A private space is readable again",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01901-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "Every command works against a private space again, and a 404 says which kind it was",
          "detail": "Making a space private broke every command that reads it. `a2a doctor`, `a2a html`, `a2a serve` and `a2a space update` all failed with GitHub's `Repository not found`, and the same `doctor` run reported the repository's visibility correctly on the line above — because that check asks the authenticated API while the mirror sync ran git with no credential at all. Both lines were honest; together they were nonsense. The mirror sync never took a credential. That cost nothing while every space was public, which is why it survived: a tokenless fetch of a public repository succeeds, and the whole local test corpus runs against local repositories where authentication is meaningless. Nothing could observe the missing argument until a real space went private. Every git call that contacts a remote now carries the resolved credential, built in one place, and the mirror sync refuses to be the exception: a source gate holds every remote-contacting verb inside the one function that takes a credential, so the next such call cannot be added without one. Reads degrade rather than fail when no credential resolves — a public space and a first `a2a connect` still work with nothing configured — while a write with no credential fails loudly, as it always did. A fetch that still 404s is no longer relayed verbatim. GitHub answers \"does not exist\" and \"you may not see it\" with the same words, so `a2a doctor` now names which one it was, using the fact it already had: when the API can read the repository with the same credential, the report says the repository EXISTS and is private or internal; when no credential resolved at all, it names that, the `A2A_TOKEN_\u003cSPACE\u003e` variable and the machine config file to put one in. A public repository gets no explanation invented for it — there the 404 means something else, such as a mistyped URL. The credential is resolved at most once per command and is never written to disk: the per-invocation authorization header is passed ahead of the git subcommand precisely so git cannot persist it into the mirror's own config, which is machine-shared across every project.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor",
            "a2a html",
            "a2a serve",
            "a2a space update",
            "a2a connect",
            "a2a sync",
            "space mirror",
            "MCP"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update the binary. A private space additionally needs a credential the binary can resolve: either `A2A_TOKEN_\u003cSPACE\u003e` in the environment or a `credentials:` entry for that space in the machine config. `a2a doctor` names both when it cannot find one.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a doctor"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.2",
      "released": "2026-08-05",
      "headline": "Configuring a private space needs no new token, and doctor stops asking for one repeatedly",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01902-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A space's credential is resolved once per command instead of once per check",
          "detail": "Every command resolved its credential separately at each place that needed one. `a2a doctor` needs one at seven points — the credentials check, the mirror fetch, the visibility, auto-merge, stuck-PR and CODEOWNERS reads, and the scaffolding comparison — and each ran inside a loop over connected spaces, so a project with two spaces resolved fourteen times per run. That was invisible with an `env:` credential, which is a variable read. It is not invisible with a `cmd:` credential, which runs a command: with the common `cmd:gh auth token` entry, one `a2a doctor` launched `gh` fourteen times. Resolution is now memoised for the lifetime of a command, keyed so that different spaces still resolve to different credentials — a space's credential is its own, and only asking the same space twice was waste. A credential that fails to resolve is also remembered, so an unusable configuration produces one failed attempt rather than seven. The credential is held in memory for that command only and is never written anywhere.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor",
            "a2a sync",
            "a2a connect",
            "a2a serve",
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to configure. A command that already worked behaves the same and does less work.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01902-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A private space can be configured with the GitHub login you already have",
          "detail": "When a space needs a credential, `a2a doctor` and the setup documentation asked for a `credentials:` entry in the machine config without showing what one looks like — so a reader with `gh` already authenticated would reasonably conclude they had to create a new personal access token. They do not. A credential entry may be `cmd:gh auth token`, which runs that command and uses its output, reusing the GitHub login already on the machine; `env:\u003cVARIABLE\u003e` reads a token from the environment instead. Both forms are now named where the question actually arises: in doctor's own message when no credential resolves, in the troubleshooting page's `credentials` row, and in the participant onboarding step where the credential is first configured.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor",
            "machine config",
            "agent skill"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A space that already resolves a credential is unaffected. A space that does not now gets told the form that requires nothing new.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.3",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "Commands that read GitHub stop running out of anonymous requests",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01903-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "`a2a update` no longer fails with a rate-limit error while you are signed in to GitHub",
          "detail": "Checking for a release read GitHub's API without identifying itself, even on a machine already signed in through the GitHub CLI. Unidentified requests share a budget of 60 per hour for the whole IP address, with everything else on it, so an ordinary day of GitHub work could exhaust the budget and then `a2a update` answered \"403 API rate limit exceeded\" — with no way to tell from the message that the fix was simply to be identified. That read now uses the GitHub CLI's own login when no update token is configured, which raises the same budget to 5000 per hour. It is the login already on the machine: nothing to create, nothing to configure. Four other commands read releases the same way and are fixed by the same change — the update notice behind the status line, `a2a sync`'s update check, and the notification companion installer. Downloading the release itself deliberately did not change. It goes through the public download URL, which is unmetered, and it switches to the authenticated path only when an update token is explicitly exported — because that is an operator choosing an identity, while a CLI login that merely exists is not that choice.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a update",
            "a2a sync",
            "status line",
            "a2a notifications install"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to configure if `gh auth login` has been run on this machine. Otherwise `A2A_UPDATE_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN` or `GITHUB_TOKEN` still work as before, and an unidentified check still works until the shared budget runs out.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01903-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "Participant avatars come back and stay",
          "detail": "Avatars could vanish from the dashboard while their images sat in the local cache, and no amount of syncing brought them back. Two separate things combined. The cache record format had moved on, so images stored by an older version were correctly ignored — that is safe on its own, because the cache is disposable and a refresh refills it. But the refresh resolved each GitHub account without identifying itself, on the same 60-per-hour shared budget as above, so on a busy machine it was refused and there was nothing to refill with. Discarded, and unrecoverable. The only sign was one advisory line in `a2a doctor`, which is not where anyone looks while staring at a dashboard that lost its faces. Account resolution is now identified the same way the release check is. Downloading the image itself stays unidentified on purpose: it comes from a content network that does not need a token and should not be given one.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "a2a serve",
            "a2a sync",
            "dashboard",
            "a2a doctor"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Run `a2a sync` once after updating to refill the cache in the current format; the dashboard shows the images again on its next build. Until then every surface keeps the participant's initials, as it already did.\n",
            "detect": [
              "a2a doctor"
            ],
            "run": [
              "a2a update --yes",
              "a2a sync"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01903-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a space update` stops telling you to change a setting you already changed",
          "detail": "The command prints a prerequisite about renaming the space's required status check, and stated it as a fact: that the pull request it just opened cannot merge until that rename happens. It cannot know that. a2a never calls repository administration APIs — there is deliberately no branch-protection capability anywhere in it — so the line is printed every time regardless of how the space is configured. For a space that was migrated long ago the sentence was simply wrong, and it would have said the same thing on every future run. A prerequisite that is wrong for most readers is one they learn to skip past, including on the occasion it is right. It now reads as a condition, and says plainly that a2a cannot check it for you.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a space update"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Wording only; the command does the same work and still changes no repository setting.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.4",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "The website publishes again",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01904-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a2ahub.dev shows the current release again after five days on an old one",
          "detail": "The public site had been serving the 0.18.2 release since 2026-08-01. Four releases were tagged, built and published in between, each with its own GitHub Release page, and the site announced none of them — so anyone reading a2ahub.dev saw a version list that ended a week ago and install instructions to match. The site is rebuilt and published by a workflow that runs the web test suite first. A change on 2026-08-04 made two of those tests import a TypeScript module that the test runner could not load, so the suite failed on every subsequent build, deterministically. The failure was loud in CI and invisible to the release process: nothing between cutting a tag and announcing it ever looked at whether the site had actually published. Both are fixed. The test runner now handles that import, and cutting a release refuses to start while the site's publish workflow is failing — a release that cannot reach the site is now something you are told before tagging, not something noticed days later. No product behaviour changed. This release exists to move the website. Verified as presentation-only rather than assumed: the exact published difference against 0.19.3 is release prose, version pins, one test file, one release-control script and a test-runner flag — no non-test Go source at all. It therefore ran no live GitHub matrix, which for this content could observe nothing; the last full matrix ran on 2026-08-05, and the provider rows it covers remain as described in the standing known issue.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2ahub.dev",
            "release process"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The binary is identical in behaviour to 0.19.3; update only if you skipped it.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.5",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "The website publishes again, and a broken site now fails before the tag",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01905-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a2ahub.dev is current again after five days on an old release",
          "detail": "The public site had been serving the 0.18.2 release since 2026-08-01. Five releases were tagged and published in between, each with its own GitHub Release page, and the site announced none of them. The site is rebuilt by a workflow that runs the web test suite first, and that suite had two independent faults. One was an import the test runner could not load, added on 2026-08-04. The other was older and quieter: two tests read a file that the build generates, but the suite ran before anything generated it — so the tests passed for anyone whose working copy still held the file from a previous build, and failed on every clean checkout, which is what continuous integration always is. Both are fixed, and the release process no longer finds out afterwards. Cutting a release refuses to start while the site's publish workflow is failing, and the candidate that gets promoted now runs the site's own build first — the same command the publish workflow runs, against the same clean shape. A site that cannot build now stops a release instead of quietly not updating. No product behaviour changed. This release exists to move the website. Verified as presentation-only rather than assumed: the exact published difference against 0.19.4 is release prose, version pins and a build script — no non-test Go source. It therefore ran no live GitHub matrix, which for this content could observe nothing; the last full matrix ran on 2026-08-05, and the provider rows it covers remain as described in the standing known issue.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2ahub.dev",
            "release process"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The binary is identical in behaviour to 0.19.3; update only if you skipped it.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.6",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "A first contract can be published again: the template you are shown is the one publication accepts",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01906-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "`a2a template show contract` printed a shape `a2a contract publish` refuses",
          "detail": "Publishing a first contract into a space running 0.19.x had no working path at all, and every gate before the irreversible step said it did. `a2a template show contract` returned the older envelope/v1 template, while `a2a new contract` had moved to envelope/v2 — which carries a top-level `artifacts:` block naming every schema and fixture the contract publishes. An author who followed the SHOWN template wrote a descriptor without that block. It validated clean, submitted, passed the space's own CI, and auto-merged to main. Only then did `a2a contract preflight` refuse it, permanently, with a message naming a requirement that appeared in no shipped document and that no flag produced. The contract was in the space's history and could not be published. `template show` now prints the generation fresh authoring actually renders, and `template list` names that generation per type, so a json-schema contract — the format the template itself defaults to — is inspected in the shape it will be written and published in. `--envelope-schema envelope/v1` still prints the older template deliberately, for a space that has not raised its floor. The scope is stated because it is not total: an openapi or proto3 contract is still authored at envelope/v1, since the declared carried set requires a schema and fixtures those formats cannot supply. Those formats therefore have NO publishable shape in a space at floor 0.19.0 or above, which is now said out loud by the refusal rather than discovered — the open gap is tracked as fb-20260802-e6d436. The gate that was supposed to catch this drift existed and was looking in the wrong place: it compared the shipped authoring pages against a fixed templates directory rather than against the binary, so it stayed green while the pages documented a shape the tool no longer wrote. It now asks `a2a template show`, which is the only comparand that cannot fall behind the product. Reported through `a2a feedback` as fb-20260806-3539ac, from a real first publication.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a template show",
            "a2a template list",
            "a2a new contract",
            "a2a contract publish"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do for an existing contract that already publishes. If you have a contract stuck at preflight, see the next entry.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01906-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A contract that cannot be published is now refused at the pull request, not after the merge",
          "detail": "A space's `min_binary_version` decides how a contract publishes: at 0.19.0 or above the descriptor must be `schema: envelope/v2` and declare every carried file under `artifacts:`; below it, the older fixed `schema/** + fixtures/**` tree is what travels and no `artifacts:` block is allowed. Nothing checked the descriptor against its own space's floor until publication, which is after the merge — so the one mismatch that can never be recovered by retrying was also the one nothing warned about. `a2a validate --ci` now refuses that mismatch on the pull request, in both directions, naming the profile the floor selects and the exact correction. A space still below the floor is unaffected: its existing contracts keep publishing the tree they always did. The publication refusal itself also changed. It used to name an internal mode and stop; it now states which floor the space is on, which profile that selects, what the descriptor must carry, and the command that corrects a descriptor that has already merged.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate --ci",
            "a2a contract preflight",
            "a2a contract publish",
            "space CI"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "If a contract is already merged and refused at preflight, correct the descriptor in your own staged candidate (`.a2a/staging/\u003csystem\u003e/provides/\u003cslug\u003e/contract.md`) and pass that directory: `a2a contract preflight \u003cXC-id\u003e --version \u003cv\u003e --staging \u003cdir\u003e`. The staged descriptor overlays the landed one, so no second submit is needed. This was verified against a real stuck contract as far as preflight, which is the step that was refusing: preflight now produces the publication plan. The publish that follows it was read from the code path, not run against a live space in this release. `reference/contract-versions.md` carries the full procedure.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01906-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a feedback submit` uses the GitHub CLI login every other command already uses",
          "detail": "`a2a init` seeds a space's credential as `cmd:gh auth token` whenever a `gh` login is available, so on a machine with working `gh` every space write authenticates itself. Feedback was the one surface that did not: it required `A2A_FEEDBACK_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` or `GH_TOKEN` and refused otherwise, so filing a report meant exporting a token by hand on a machine that was already logged in. Feedback has no `a2a init` of its own to seed the reference during, so the login is now consulted at resolve time instead, after the environment names and before refusing. An explicit token still wins.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a feedback submit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `gh auth login` is now enough to file feedback; existing token environment variables keep working and keep precedence.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01906-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "The shipped contract authoring page documents the contract the tool writes",
          "detail": "`skill/a2ahub/reference/authoring/contract.md` still carried the envelope/v1 shape, including `version: 1.0.0` — which `reference/contract-versions.md` itself says publish refuses. It is now the descriptor `a2a new contract` renders, and the same reference gained the section that was missing entirely: what the `artifacts:` inventory is, which roles are required, which profile a space's floor selects, and how to correct a descriptor that already merged. The strings this release's refusals use appeared in zero lines of the shipped manual before it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "skill manual",
            "a2ahub.dev docs"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Re-run `a2a skill install` (or `a2a skill link`) so the installed manual carries the corrected contract page and the new authoring section.\n",
            "run": [
              "a2a skill install"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.7",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "A same-day fix for a v0.19.6 regression, plus three more places the same defect was hiding",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01907-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "`a2a template list` prints one type per line again",
          "detail": "v0.19.6 added a second, tab-separated column to `a2a template list` naming each type's envelope generation. It read well to a human and broke every machine that consumes the listing: `for t in $(a2a template list)` word-splits on whitespace, so the loop ran `a2a template show envelope/v1` and failed. a2ahub's own `skill-drift` CI job does exactly that, and went red on the release commit itself. The bare listing is a machine surface, and its contract is one type per line whether or not anything had written that down. It is restored, and a test now refuses any listing line carrying interior whitespace — running `template show` on every line, so a word-split cannot pass. The generation is still available, in a representation nothing splits: `a2a template list --json` emits one row per type with its `type` and `envelope_schema`. That is where a field belongs when the plain output already has consumers. This was our regression, shipped and taken back the same day. It is exactly the class v0.19.6 existed to fix — an output surface changing under a consumer that could not see it coming.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a template list"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "If you scripted against v0.19.6's two-column output, switch to `a2a template list --json`; the plain listing is one column again, as it was through v0.19.5.\n",
            "run": [
              "a2a update"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01907-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a contract preflight` no longer plans a publication `publish` will refuse",
          "detail": "On a space whose `min_binary_version` is below 0.19.0, `a2a contract preflight \u003cid\u003e --bump minor` printed a target version and a plan digest, and `a2a contract publish` with the identical arguments refused immediately — below that floor there is no published history to bump from safely, so the version has to be named explicitly. The rule lived only inside `publish`, even though it is a pure function of two values both commands already read the same way. It now lives in one place both call, so the verb whose stated purpose is to preview the exact publication plan cannot preview one that will not execute. A test asserts the two AGREE rather than checking each separately, because each was individually defensible and only their disagreement was wrong.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract preflight",
            "a2a contract publish"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If preflight now refuses where it used to plan, publish was always going to refuse — pass `--version \u003csemver\u003e` instead of `--bump`.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01907-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Every space write uses the GitHub CLI login, not just `a2a feedback`",
          "detail": "v0.19.6 taught `a2a feedback submit` to fall back to the machine's `gh` login. Every REAL space write kept refusing on the same machine, which is worse than not having fixed either: it makes the remaining refusal look like a problem with that particular space. `a2a connect` seeds `cmd:gh auth token` into the machine config only ONCE, at connect time. A space connected before `gh` was set up — or a config hand-authored or copied between machines — carries a plain `env:A2A_TOKEN_\u003cID\u003e` reference and never sees the login. The seed happens once; the resolve happens on every write, and only the resolve knows what the machine looks like now. Both surfaces now resolve through one function, and a test pins them to agree on the same machine rather than checking each alone. An explicit token still wins over the login, everywhere.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a contract publish",
            "every space write",
            "a2a mcp"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `gh auth login` is now enough for a space write too; existing tokens keep working and keep precedence.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01907-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "A refused schema version says which value is accepted and what to do",
          "detail": "Three checks — on `space.yaml`, on `consumes.yaml`, and on every artifact envelope — refused with \"schema version is outside the one-cycle overlap window this binary understands\". That named neither the accepted value nor a remedy, and those words appeared in zero lines of the shipped manual or the published docs. It also collapsed two opposite causes into one sentence: a value that does not parse at all is a typo the author fixes in place, while a version this build does not accept usually means the document was written by a newer a2a and the READER should update. Both now name the accepted `schema:` value — rendered from the same source the check itself uses, so the message cannot claim a window the check does not enforce — and the correct remedy for that cause. This came out of a sweep for the same defect shape the previous release fixed: a refusal naming a precondition with no reachable means of satisfaction.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate",
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a validate --ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do; the refusals are the same refusals, better worded.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01907-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "One publication floor, not two literals that had to match by convention",
          "detail": "The submit-time carried-set check compared against one `0.19.0` constant and the publication planner against a different one. They were equal, and nothing derived either from the other — so a release bumping one and not the other would have reopened precisely the class of bug the pair exists to prevent: an early gate accepting a shape the planner refuses after the merge. The planner's constant now aliases the release-wide floor. Also removes `internal/html/docs/` — 21 committed Markdown files that no code, script or workflow read. The dashboard's Documentation tab and a2ahub.dev both render from the embedded `skill/` tree through one manifest, and have for some time; the leftover copy had already gone stale, missing a whole section of the contract-versions page. Verified by deleting it and rendering the dashboard, not by reading imports.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "space CI"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The documentation an agent reads is unchanged and comes from the same place it already did.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.8",
      "released": "2026-08-06",
      "headline": "The companion half of a contract can be published, and a first release can prove its own provenance",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01908-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A contract that declares a companion artifact can be published",
          "detail": "contract-set-v2 requires every companion role — errors, vocabulary, limits, changelog, example, other — to live under `artifacts/`, and the envelope schema enforces that with a role-conditional path pattern, so a companion has no other legal location. Two other surfaces of that same rule did not know the directory exists. The space path layout recognised the descriptor, `schema/**` and `fixtures/valid|invalid/**` and nothing else, so a companion was not classified as carried contract data. The submit validator then treated it as an envelope draft and tried to parse frontmatter out of a JSON file, and `a2a contract publish` died with \"missing or malformed frontmatter delimiters\" naming the companion — while `a2a validate` and `a2a contract preflight` had both passed. The whole companion half of the profile was unpublishable, and only the irreversible verb said so. There was a second half: with no forward constructor for the directory, the collector `a2a submit` uses to carry a staged contract's files had no `artifacts/` arm either, so a declared companion was staged, declared, and then simply not carried — the descriptor naming a file the space never received. Both directions are fixed together, in the one file that holds them, because a directory must not be legal in one direction and unknown in the other. Reported as fb-20260806-c6ad38 from a real first publication.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish",
            "a2a submit",
            "space layout"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you removed a companion to get a contract published, you can declare it again on the next version.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01908-1",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a contract verify-export` computes the export-source-v1 digest before the first publish",
          "detail": "A code-generated contract asserts its provenance in `generated_from.source_digest`, and that value must be the export-source-v1 digest. Nothing computed it for a candidate that was not published yet: the only verb that could resolved a published version first, so it was unreachable for exactly the case that needs it — a producer's FIRST release. The remaining path was to attempt publication, read the computed value out of the refusal, and paste it back. That makes the assertion self-fulfilling: the field exists so a producer can prove the published export matches their code, and a number copied from a2a's own computation proves only that a2a agrees with itself. The sanction was backwards too — omit `generated_from` and publication proceeds; only the author honest enough to declare a code-generated contract was stopped. `a2a contract verify-export --local \u003cdir\u003e \u003cXC-id\u003e`, with no version, now computes the digest over a local candidate and reports it against whatever the descriptor asserts. The `\u003cXC-id\u003e@\u003cversion\u003e` form is unchanged. This is deliberately a CHECK rather than a source, so the documentation carries the load-bearing half: `reference/contract-versions.md` now defines the profile — declared schema and fixture entries only, descriptor and `artifacts/` companions excluded, path-to-digest pairs combined — so a producer can implement it in their own generator and have something independent to compare. Reported as fb-20260806-eb224e.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract verify-export",
            "skill manual"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "If you generate contracts, implement export-source-v1 in your generator from the definition now in reference/contract-versions.md, and use this command to check the two agree before publishing.\n",
            "run": [
              "a2a contract verify-export --local \u003cdir\u003e \u003cXC-id\u003e"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01908-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "The carried set's four path roots are documented where the inventory is built",
          "detail": "Each `artifacts:` role admits exactly one root — `schema/` for schema, `fixtures/valid/` and `fixtures/invalid/` for the two fixture roles, and `artifacts/` for every companion. The restriction was enforced by an embedded schema pattern, hinted at in one template comment, absent from the page where an author actually builds the inventory, and reported as an untyped pattern failure on an array index that named neither the role nor the root it required. `reference/contract-versions.md` now states the four pairs as a table, and says plainly that a file living elsewhere in a producer's release tree — a fixture-suite manifest at `fixtures/manifest.json` is the case that comes up — has to move, so the published set is not always a path-for-path mirror of the tree that generated it. Reported as fb-20260806-2af06c.\n",
          "affects": [
            "skill manual",
            "a2ahub.dev docs"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Re-run `a2a skill install` (or `a2a skill link`) to pick up the roots table and the export-source-v1 definition.\n",
            "run": [
              "a2a skill install"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.9",
      "released": "2026-08-07",
      "headline": "A settled contract stops asking you to do something about it",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "\"Whose move is it\" is one answer now, and a published contract is nobody's move",
          "detail": "A contract you published and your consumer adopted sat in the thread view and on the dashboard as an open item waiting on you, forever, with an agent prompt offered for a turn that was not yours. Nothing was wrong with the contract. The reason it never cleared is that two different questions had been collapsed into one. An artifact can be ALIVE — still existing, still able to move — without anything being PENDING on anyone. A published contract is the standing case: its owner may always publish a successor or deprecate it, and neither is a move someone is waiting for. The thread view computed \"who is waiting\" by listing every legal next move and naming whoever may make it, so the owner's permanent right to publish read as a permanent debt owed to themselves. `a2a inbox --actionable` answered the same question separately, with its own five conditions, so the two surfaces could and did disagree. There is now one relation, asked by every surface, that answers who owes the next move, which transition they owe, and WHY — and \"nobody\" is a verdict it has to justify rather than something that falls out of a predicate. Its rows come from the domain's own per-type transition tables, and a gate refuses to let a state exist that no row covers, so a new state cannot quietly inherit the wrong answer. What you will see change: settled work drops off open items and stops counting toward a thread's open count, so `settled` and `your_move` mean what they say; `a2a thread --json` gained `expected_transition` and `why` on each open item; and a plain announcement that requested no acknowledgement no longer sits in `--actionable` waiting for one. (A DEPRECATION announcement is the exception, and RN-01909-3 explains why: a consumer registered against the contract being deprecated still owes that acknowledgement, flag or no flag, because it is what unblocks the retire.) The dashboard's agent prompt is gated on the same relation. It used to be built from every move the protocol would ACCEPT, so a settled contract handed an agent \"publish, deprecate\" as though one of them were due — and the thread view and the exchange rows built it separately, so the same artifact could prompt on one surface and stay quiet on the other. One gate now answers for both, and it asks whether the move is owed, not whether it is legal. Nothing was removed from the record. Every legal move a settled artifact still has is listed exactly as before under `next_actions` — the change is that it is no longer counted as a debt, and no longer read to an agent as an instruction. Everything that happened is still in the transcript, permanently, because it is in git.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a thread",
            "a2a inbox --actionable",
            "a2a html",
            "a2a outbox"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Threads you had been watching sit open for no reason should settle by themselves on the next read; if one still names a system you think is done, its `why` now says which rule put it there.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A rejected handoff tells its producer to resubmit, instead of disappearing",
          "detail": "When a receiver rejects a handoff, the protocol puts the producer on the hook: resubmit as a new handoff superseding the rejected one. That move was never reported. The thread view decided which artifacts to consider before it asked whose move it was, and its list of live states for a handoff stopped at `acknowledged` — so a rejected handoff was dropped from open items entirely, and the system that owed the resubmission was simply never told. The rule was always right; nothing ever asked it. A rejected handoff now appears as an open item pending on its producer, with `supersede` as the transition owed. Found by a new conformance path that drives a real rejection and then asks whose move it is. It could not have been found by testing the rejection transition on its own, because the transition was never the broken part.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a thread",
            "a2a inbox --actionable",
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If a handoff of yours was rejected and then went quiet, it will reappear as yours to answer on the next read — the record kept it the whole time, only the reminder was missing.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Fulfilling a requirement no longer breaks the thread you fulfilled it on",
          "detail": "A requirement's only prescribed fulfilment path is to author a response naming it as parent and submit that. Doing so permanently flagged the requirement's own thread as carrying an illegal transition, and a flagged conversation never renders clean again. Nothing was wrong with the response or the submission. The thread reader gathered a response's events into its parent's stream, and then evaluated them against the PARENT's type — so the response's own `submit` was asked of a requirement, which has no such move. Only two events genuinely belong to a parent (a verification and a dispute), and now only those two cross over. The same wave also corrected the requirement's own pending answer. It had been telling the RECEIVING system to emit the satisfy event, which the protocol reserves for the requester — so the surface named a move the tool would then refuse. Before a response exists, the receiver owes the work (and the tool says so plainly, without pretending it is a transition on the requirement); once one lands, the requester owes the satisfy. Both were found by conformance paths that walk a whole requirement exchange end to end, not by testing either transition alone — neither transition was the broken part.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a thread",
            "a2a inbox --actionable",
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do, and nothing to re-file. The flag was computed at read time, never stored, so an affected requirement thread reads clean on the next look; the response you already submitted stands.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Three answers about whose move it is, corrected: deprecation acks, departed systems, and human-gated approvals",
          "detail": "Two independent reviews read the pending-move rules against the domain specification, and each finding was then re-checked against the source before being believed. Three were real, and all three had the same shape — the rule was right and the answer was not. A deprecation announcement that did not explicitly request acknowledgements told its registered consumers they owed nothing, while the retire check refused to retire the contract until those same consumers acknowledged. Two parts of the tool disagreed about one question. Requesting acknowledgement governs who is addressed by NAME; a system registered as a consumer of the contract being deprecated owes the acknowledgement regardless, because that is the acknowledgement the retire waits on. A counterparty that has LEFT the space kept owing its move forever. It can no longer write, so no legal act could ever clear the row, and no agent had an action that removed it. Such a system is now excluded, and the pending move transfers to the sender as a cancel-or-re-route decision — the item stays visible, with an owner who can actually act on it, instead of freezing. Approving a decision is a human gate: the event has to arrive through a pull request under the approving human's own account, and an agent that emits it directly is ignored. The pending answer named the approver's system without saying any of that, so an agent reading it would try a move the tool refuses. Open items now carry `human_gate` when the move they name is gated, so an agent can escalate instead of failing. `a2a html` carries it too now — see RN-01909-4; when this entry was first written it did not, and saying so was better than a claim the dashboard could not honour.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a thread",
            "a2a inbox --actionable",
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If a deprecation of yours seemed stuck — the retire refusing while nobody appeared to owe anything — the consumer blocking it is now named. Items owed by a departed system move to you on the next read.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The dashboard says which version was published, who adopted it, and why nobody owes a move",
          "detail": "The local dashboard was answering less than the tool knew, in four separate places, and every one of them had the data already. A contract with three publications rendered three identical lines — \"published the contract\", three times — with nothing saying which version each created and every link resolving to the version-less contract. The events carried their version the whole time; only the view dropped it. Rows now read \"published contract 1.0.1\", and the one that publishes the contract ITSELF, carrying no version, is finally distinguishable from the two that publish versions. A counterparty adopting a contract was invisible on its thread. It now appears beside the artifact, naming each consumer and the version it actually pinned — which is the number that says whether a deprecation still has anybody to wait for. It is deliberately not a timeline entry: adoption is a registry fact, not something that happened on this thread, and the timeline only ever shows what happened. Open items now carry the reason behind them. \"Nobody owes a move here\" is a conclusion the tool is willing to justify, and the dashboard was showing the conclusion while withholding the justification. It also now marks a move as human-gated where it is one, so an agent reading the dashboard stops being told to make a move the protocol would ignore. Finally, work-progress rows show the same faces and profile links as every other surface, and the cards on the overview page match the ones in Threads, Contracts and Exchange in heading size, padding and inner structure.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "a2a dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The dashboard is rendered fresh from your local cache every time, so the next `a2a html` shows all of it.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "Who acted is now read from the environment, not typed in by the agent",
          "detail": "Every artifact and event records an actor, permanently, in a log nobody can rewrite. Until now that field was whatever the caller said it was, and two different wrong answers were reaching it. The first was a person's OS username recorded under a claim that a machine acted — `kind: agent, name: yuranikolaev` — because the OS user was the last fallback and nothing above it had answered. The second is worse and was found in a live space: `kind: agent, name: codex` on a contract publish that codex did not perform. The name had been typed at the command line, nothing verified it, and the event carries no provenance for it. The commit author is the synthetic identity the write funnel always stamps, so git cannot settle it either. The authoring templates invited exactly this. Eight of them shipped the line `actor: {kind: agent, name: \u003cagent-name\u003e, ...}`, and an agent asked to fill in its own name will sometimes fill in a different one. So the order is inverted. When the environment identifies the coding agent that is running, that verdict wins outright over any flag, env var, harness default or config value — an agent can be talked into naming the wrong tool, an environment cannot. Detection is wired only for vendors whose environment has actually been read on a real machine; every other vendor ships a recognized id with no detector, so `A2A_ACTOR_AGENT=\u003cid\u003e` records it correctly today rather than a detector guessing wrongly. An explicit `--actor-kind human` still suppresses detection, because a person saying \"this was me\" is a claim about something else entirely. The human an agent acted FOR is not written into the event at all. It is derived when reading, from the space manifest's `owners` for the acting system — where it already lives, and where it stays correct when the owner changes.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a new",
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a lifecycle verbs",
            "a2a contract",
            "a2a mcp"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Nothing to do for most invocations — the next write simply records a truer actor. Two cases are worth knowing about. If you keep a work stream open across this upgrade, its reporter identity is immutable for the life of that work id, so a checkpoint written after the upgrade can be refused for changing the reporter name; pass `--actor-name \u003cthe original\u003e` to finish it, or start a new work id. And if you author envelopes by hand, stop filling in `actor.name` — the templates no longer ask you to, and a value you type there is exactly how the wrong attribution above happened.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-6",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Three tests stopped spending a production timeout to prove the rule it enforces",
          "detail": "Nothing about the shipped tool changes. This is recorded because the files touched are production files, and a release note that stayed silent about them would leave the next reader wondering what moved. Three bounds — the cache's mirror-sync budget, the mirror lock's wait, and the git index-lock retry — could only be reached by tests that spent them. Each test asserts a SHAPE: the wait ends, and it ends with a typed contention error rather than a hang. None of them asserts the number of seconds. So the bound became a parameter whose production default is the same constant it always was, and the tests hand in a short one: 13.05s to 0.48s, 6.16s to 0.39s, 3.03s to 0.32s. Each test's upper bound was rebased onto its own budget at the same time. Left pointing at the production constants they would have become 20s and 10s ceilings on runs that finish in a third of a second — unreachable, and therefore asserting nothing. That is how a faster suite quietly becomes a weaker one.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The timeouts a2a actually enforces are unchanged; only the tests that prove them are cheaper to run.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01909-7",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "The dashboard names the agent that acted, and stops calling a healthy contract unfinished work",
          "detail": "Four things the local dashboard was saying that were not true, all found by reading the rendered page against a real space rather than its JSON. An act now shows BOTH identities. The system owner's face and name lead, and the agent that performed the write sits as a badge on the avatar with a popover naming it. Before this, one of the two was always hidden: the thread view overwrote the recorded actor with the owner, so a publish by an agent read as though a person had made it, while the overview card did the opposite and printed a bare OS username. Whether a badge appears depends on what the event recorded. Events written before this release mostly carry an OS username, which names no agent and gets no badge; every event written from now on records the detected agent, so its badge follows automatically. The network map no longer describes a published contract as unfinished work. It had been aggregating everything still OPEN — which a published contract is, since it can always be deprecated — and labelling that set with the vocabulary of things somebody owes a move on. The same panel then said «кого ждут: никого». Now the map says \"live work\", counts separately how many of those documents actually owe a move, and colours only those. A contract's thread shows who adopted it. `a2a contract adopt` writes the consumer's registry and emits no event, so a counterparty's most consequential act had no place in any chronology and a contract's thread read as its owner talking to themselves. The row is derived from the consumer's own registry and says so — it is not styled as an event, because there is no event. A contract version's package is now a collapsible tree with per-format icons instead of two flat path lists, its prose folds through the same disclosure component the rest of the dashboard uses, and an overview card with nothing to report is short instead of four lines of \"none\".\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The dashboard is rendered fresh from your local cache every time, so the next `a2a html` shows all of it. Agent badges appear on events written by this release onward; older events keep whatever actor they recorded, because the log is append-only.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.10",
      "released": "2026-08-08",
      "headline": "The domain says what a state means, and every surface reads the same answer",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-0",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "An artifact now reports what its state MEANS, and which event put it there",
          "detail": "Four surfaces each decided, separately, what a state was worth telling you about it. The dashboard did it from three lists of state names with no notion of artifact type, and being type-blind is what made them wrong. A retired contract was filed as cancelled — so an orderly retirement and an abandoned artifact rendered as the same event, when retiring a contract is how a contract's life is supposed to end. And `accepted` was one entry for every type, though on a handoff it is where the exchange ENDS (it is what a passing verification produces) and on a question it is the middle of the work. One list could not be right for both. The domain answers now, and every surface reads that answer instead of computing its own. `a2a inbox`, `a2a outbox`, `a2a thread --json` and the MCP read tools carry two new fields per artifact: `outcome` — one of open, settled, refused, withdrawn, superseded — and `terminal`, whether any move can still follow. They are deliberately different questions, and the difference is not pedantry: a rejected decision is `refused` AND non-terminal, because it can still be superseded. A failed handoff is the same shape, and the producer is the one who owes the resubmission. Alongside them, three fields answer \"since when, and who\": `state_since`, `state_by` and `state_event` name the event that produced the current state. That is NOT the artifact's latest event. A `note` changes nothing about state, and the dashboard had been reading the newest event of any kind under a \"moved\" label — so an artifact looked like it had moved when somebody had only commented. Both clocks are now carried, separately. Events themselves say which kind they are. `transition_free` marks an event that changes no state — a note anywhere, and an acknowledgement of a broadcast. It is not something a reader can work out from the transition name: acknowledging a requirement moves it, acknowledging a broadcast does not, so the same word means both. It appears on `a2a thread --json`, `a2a show --json` and the dashboard.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The new fields are additive on read surfaces. If you parse `a2a inbox --json` or `a2a thread --json`, prefer `outcome` and `terminal` over matching state names yourself — matching names is what every surface used to do, and it is what got `retired` and handoff `accepted` wrong.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-2",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "`a2a __catalog --vocabulary --json` publishes the domain's own vocabulary",
          "detail": "The hidden `__catalog` verb gained a machine-readable mode listing every outcome, every state per artifact type, and every transition name — all derived from the transition table rather than written out. It exists so a check can police what the dashboard is allowed to spell without carrying its own copy of the list. A copy is correct the day it is written and silently wrong the day a state is added, which is how `canceled` — spelled with one `l`, matching nothing the protocol can ever produce — sat inside the dashboard's own \"is this cancelled\" list until a check derived from the binary found it. The plain `a2a __catalog` output is unchanged.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. This is an internal surface; the flag is stable if you want to read it, but nothing in normal operation calls it.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The session the tool already detected now reaches the record",
          "detail": "When an agent acts, a2a can tell which agent session it is — that is how several events get correlated back to one conversation. The value was detected and then dropped on the floor: no struct on the authoring path had a field for it, no event writer carried it, and the policy check that bounds its length had nothing to check, because nothing could produce one. A session id did get written in exactly one place — the work-checkpoint path — which mints its own instead of asking detection. So the value existed twice and neither copy was the real one. Drafts and events now carry `actor.session` and `actor.model` when the environment supplies them. An explicit `--field actor.session=` still wins: naming it yourself is adding information, not contradicting the environment. One asymmetry is deliberate. `a2a submit` records the actor from the DRAFT it is submitting, not whoever ran the command — a submit event says who authored the artifact, and re-resolving would credit the wrong party.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If your harness exports a session id, new drafts and events pick it up; existing records are untouched.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "A template's own actor kind is no longer overwritten by a default nobody chose",
          "detail": "The decision template declares `kind: human` for its actor, with a note saying why: approving a decision is a human gate, so a decision usually carries a human actor. Drafting one overwrote that with `agent` every time — not because anything had decided the actor was an agent, but because `agent` is what the resolver falls back to when nothing names a kind. A fallback and a choice are different things, and the draft could not tell them apart. Now it can: an explicit `--actor-kind`, or a detected agent, still wins over whatever the template declares, and a bare default leaves the template's own value standing.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `a2a new decision` now keeps `kind: human` unless you say otherwise; every other template already declared `agent`, so nothing changes for them.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Five schema-legal envelope fields you could not actually set",
          "detail": "`effort_estimate`, `supersedes`, `origin`, `migrated_from` and — on most types — `expected_response` are legal on every artifact and appear in no template. Setting one with `--field` was refused for not being in the template, and putting it in the template was not an option either: every draft would then carry an unfilled placeholder, which submit refuses. Neither end could move, so the fields stayed unusable. What a draft may contain is the schema's answer, not the template's — a template is one convenience over the schema. `--field` now adds a key the schema allows AND that you are entitled to set, so the field appears only when you ask for it and no other draft changes. Three of the five stay unavailable, each for its own reason: `migrated_from` is stamped by the tool during a migration and is not yours to type; `origin` and `expected_response` are a list and a block, and `--field` sets single values only. The two that were genuinely yours to state — `effort_estimate` and `supersedes` — now work. A name the schema does not have is still refused, so a typo stays a typo.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `a2a new question --field origin=...` and the other four now work; drafts you do not ask for them on are unchanged.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-6",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A decision or handoff whose counterparty left the space can now be closed by its sender",
          "detail": "Three situations left a sender with nothing they could legally do. A decision waiting on approvers who have all left the space stayed `proposed` forever: approving and rejecting belong to the approvers, and the proposer had no move at all. A handoff whose receiver left stayed `submitted` or `acknowledged` the same way — including handoffs carrying committed payload bytes, which is the case that hurts. The tooling already knew what should happen. When every counterparty has left, the \"whose move is it\" answer transfers to the sender and says they owe a cancel-or-re-route decision. That was a promise the transition table could not keep: it named the sender and then refused every move the sender could make. A proposer can now withdraw or supersede their own proposed decision, and a producer can supersede a submitted or acknowledged handoff. Withdraw and supersede stay separate on purpose — \"no longer needed\" and \"replaced by this one\" are different statements, and forcing one to stand in for the other is the kind of substitution this work exists to remove.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you have a decision or handoff stuck because the other side left, `a2a withdraw` or `a2a supersede` now works on it.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-7",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "An artifact addressed to several systems now lets every one of them act",
          "detail": "A requirement, contract, decision or announcement may be addressed to more than one system — nothing in the schema limits it, and only the four exchange types do. When one was, the tooling told all of the addressees the move was theirs and then accepted it from the first one only. The other named systems saw `your_move: true`, tried the move they had been told they owed, and were refused. One `a2a thread --json` object could carry three systems under \"waiting on\" and one under \"next actions\" at the same time. Every addressee named on an artifact can now act on it. The same narrowing sat on a second, quieter path: attaching a note. A note is open to either party by design — it changes no state, and commenting is how a party says something the transitions have no word for. On a multi-addressee artifact only the first addressee could leave one, so a system that had just been allowed to acknowledge was refused when it tried to explain itself. The check that exists to catch precisely this disagreement could not see it: its probe addressed a single system, so the two sides agreed trivially. It now probes three.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you were named on a multi-addressee artifact and your acknowledgement, decline or note was refused, it works now.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-9",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A blocked question or work request can now be cancelled",
          "detail": "A blocked exchange is the one most likely to be abandoned — the work stopped because something outside it did not arrive — and it was the one state from which cancelling was refused. Every other live state accepted it: draft, submitted, acknowledged, accepted, in progress. So a requester who no longer needed a blocked request had two options, both wrong. Wait for a party that is by definition not the one who can unblock it, or reach for `supersede`, which states \"replaced by this other artifact\" — a different claim, recorded permanently, about work that was simply no longer needed. `a2a cancel` now works from `blocked` on questions and work requests.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. A blocked request you had left open because you could not close it honestly can now be cancelled.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-8",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a inbox` and `a2a outbox` now say who owes the next move, which move, and why",
          "detail": "The answer was already being computed for these commands — it decides whether `--actionable` lists an artifact at all — and then thrown away. So `a2a thread --json` showed the conclusion and the reasoning behind it, while `a2a inbox --json` showed neither for the same artifact. Both now carry `waiting_on`, `expected_transition` and `why`. The last one matters most: \"nobody owes anything\" is a claim the tooling has to justify rather than something that falls out of a filter, and a surface that shows a verdict while withholding its reason republishes the ambiguity the verdict exists to remove.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The three fields are additive. If you were parsing `--actionable` to guess why something appeared, read `why` instead.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-10",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A deprecation you are registered for no longer disappears from your exchange feed",
          "detail": "When a contract you consume is deprecated, the announcement is addressed to you two ways: by the recipient list frozen at deprecation time, and by the live registry of who actually consumes the contract. The second is the one that matters — a system that adopted the contract after the announcement was written is not in the frozen list, and `a2a contract retire` still refuses to retire until that system has acknowledged. The exchange feed used a third rule of its own. It required the announcement's `ack_requested` flag before considering anyone at all, and the registry-matched half of the addressing carries no such qualifier. So a deprecation published without the flag was filed as delivered and archived out of the consumer's feed, while the retire it was blocking named that same consumer as the reason. The feed asks the same relation as every other surface now, so the announcement stays in front of the system that owes the acknowledgement. Two more answers stop disagreeing as a consequence: `a2a inbox --json` now justifies \"nobody owes anything\" the way `a2a thread --json` already did, and the departed-counterparty verdict — the sender owes a cancel-or-re-route decision when every addressee has left — is the same on both.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If a deprecation vanished from your exchange view while a retire was blocked on your acknowledgement, it is visible again.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-11",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A response now says who owes the next move, on every surface that lists it",
          "detail": "A response artifact answers somebody else's question or work request, and it carries no addressee of its own — so who owes the verify is resolved from the PARENT it answers. `a2a thread --json` did that. `a2a inbox --json` did not: it had no parent in hand, and the answer degraded to \"nobody owes anything\" rather than to an error. One artifact, two answers, on two commands reading the same data. Both surfaces resolve the parent now. `a2a outbox --json` also carries `waiting_on`, `expected_transition` and `why` for the first time — they were promised when the fields were added and only the inbox had been wired. Alongside, one string stops reaching readers. When an artifact is in a state no obligation is defined for — a declined request you are still listed on — the justification read \"this should be unreachable\" and named an internal function. That sentence was written for a surface where it was true and became visible on one where it is not; the state is now described plainly, and the claim it used to make is proven by a test instead of narrated to a user.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you parse `a2a outbox --json`, three fields that were always absent may now be present; they are the same fields `a2a inbox --json` already carried.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-12",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A work request can carry the bytes it is about, and a reference that does not resolve is refused",
          "detail": "A request that is *about* some bytes had no way to carry them unless those bytes were a dataset conforming to a pinned contract and were answering a specific work request. Anything else — a transcript, a bundle accompanying a question, a sample nobody is promising to judge — could only be described in prose with a digest quoted beside it. A reader then had a pointer they could not resolve, and the party who wrote it never found out. `schemas/envelope/v2/work_request.schema.json` is new and declares an `attachments[]` block: a content-addressed reference, its `sha256:` digest, an optional contract to conform to, what verification is being asked for (`required`, `offered` or `none` — and `none` means no verdict is defined, which is a different claim from unverified), and how long the bytes are retained. `a2a attach \u003cdraft\u003e --from \u003cfile-or-dir\u003e --verification required|offered|none` writes the entry, mints the content-addressed reference and computes the digest. `verification` has no default and the command refuses without it: defaulting it would claim somebody promised a verdict on bytes nobody promised to judge, and separating that claim from the bytes themselves is the whole point. And the reference now has to resolve. `a2a validate` refuses an artifact whose BODY names a `sha256:` that no declared attachment carries — locally, before any pull request, which is what makes the difference: the failure this is written from took six days to surface because nothing local could see it. The set of digests checked is scanned out of the body rather than declared, so it cannot drift from what an author actually wrote. A body that merely lists what looks like a file tree with nothing attached gets a WARNING, never a refusal — only checkable facts refuse. What does NOT work yet: `a2a new work_request` still writes `envelope/v1`, so a draft carrying attachments has to be authored by hand for now; and three read surfaces still need teaching — `verification: none` renders as a missing package rather than \"no verdict is defined\", a lapsed retention reads as a failed fetch rather than a dead reference, and submit does not yet prove an attachment is fetchable from the space rather than from the author's machine. The field lands on a schema that is itself NEW rather than widening one already shipped, deliberately. Every released binary carries its own embedded copy of the schemas, and each concrete type closes itself against unknown properties — so adding a field to a schema an older binary already knows would make it refuse a legal document it believes it understands. An unknown TYPE is refused honestly instead, and only for an author who chose the newer generation.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing `envelope/v1` work requests are untouched and stay valid, and the new refusal only fires on a body that names a digest — an artifact that names none is unaffected. Before a space can accept `envelope/v2` work requests it must be running a binary that knows the type: the CI workflow a space pins decides that, not the author's local install.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-13",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A blocked request names who is actually blocking it",
          "detail": "When work is blocked, somebody has to unblock it — and the tooling named the wrong party. The answer was always \"the system this was addressed to\", regardless of who the block was actually waiting on. So a requester blocked on a third party was told the recipient owed the move, the recipient was told the same thing, and neither was the party who could resolve it. A partial answer can now name its blocker (`blocked_by`), and that name is what the tooling reports. An artifact that names nobody answers exactly as before. One limit, stated because it is real: the party now named still has no move of their own to make — `unblock` remains the recipient's act. So the record stops pointing at the wrong system, and routing an act to the right one is a further step.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing blocked artifacts are unaffected until a response names a blocker.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-15",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A partial answer can say what is unmet, what blocks it, and on whose authority",
          "detail": "Answering \"partially\" was a single word with no way to qualify it. There was nowhere to record which acceptance criteria were not met, what would unblock them, how much was attempted, or — the case that forced this — that every criterion WAS answered while the supplier is not entitled to make the values binding. That last one had no expression at all: an author had to either misreport a met criterion as unmet, or drop the caveat and overclaim. `envelope/v2` responses carry `unmet[]`, `blocked_by`, `attempted`, `standing` and `residue`. A `partial` must now carry at least one of them — naming none is refused, because \"partial\" alone tells a reader nothing they can act on. `unmet[]` is an INDEX into the parent's acceptance criteria, never a restatement in prose. A restatement drifts from the criterion the moment either is edited, and then two documents disagree about what was asked. And a closure now has to account for what it closes over: ending an exchange that carries an unmet criterion requires saying where that criterion went. Both anchor cases for this were work requests closed three weeks early with open items named in the closing note — items that a week later existed on no surface at all. Two of these refusals shipped able to be described and unable to fire, and that is worth saying because this note described them first: the rule that checks an unmet index against the parent needed a lookup the tooling could not do, and the closure rule never received the closing event. Both are wired now, so `a2a submit` refuses what this paragraph says it refuses. An unmet index that names a criterion the parent does not have is caught on the author's machine; note that at merge time the index check does not yet run, so it warns you early rather than guarding the gate.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `envelope/v1` responses are unchanged and stay valid. The new refusals apply to `envelope/v2` responses only.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-16",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A work request can declare what it IS — adoptable, pinnable, or explicitly none of those",
          "detail": "Whether something is a binding interface, a draft to react to, or a bundle nobody is promising to keep, was carried in prose — and in the case this comes from, in three different places, none of which owned it: a fenced block, the body of a field that answers a different question, and an acceptance criterion binding both parties. Someone looked for a field in three places and found none. `binding` is a field now. It is either the explicit sentinel `none` or an object saying what class the artifact is, what compatibility is claimed, and whether it may be adopted or pinned. The sentinel is not a shorthand for leaving it out: declaring nothing and declaring that NOTHING IS CLAIMED are different statements, and only the second is something a reader can rely on. A declared `compatibility_status: none` is held to the same standard as the bare sentinel — it cannot present as adoptable and pinnable while claiming no compatibility.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The field is optional and `envelope/v1` work requests are unaffected.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-17",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "The loops route to every instrument the protocol actually has",
          "detail": "Five acts existed in the protocol and in no loop an agent reads: withdrawing, cancelling, the responder's half of a dispute, starting accepted work, and unblocking. Two more shipped in this release — attaching bytes, and naming what blocks a partial answer — and needed the same. `start` was the worst of them, and not for being missing: the loops use `a2a work start` nearby for an unrelated command family, so the silence taught that no lifecycle `start` exists between accepting work and answering it. The autonomy section is rewritten to what the tooling can actually answer. One of the five human gates is machine-readable and four are not, and it now says so instead of implying a clean split — an agent told it can check something it cannot will either invent a check or stop asking. Alongside, a coverage check: every combination of artifact type, role and phase either has a step or is declared empty with a reason read off the transition table. Its first run found that the loops had never explained how to abandon or retire anything.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. This is guidance an agent reads, not behaviour it invokes; no command changed.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The reason code an agent is required to supply when refusing now reaches the reader",
          "detail": "Declining a request requires a machine-readable `reason_code` — the MCP tool refuses the call without one, and both event schemas define the field. It was written into the event and then read by nobody: the cache that builds every read surface never decoded it, so the only thing a receiver could act on was the prose note beside it. That is exactly the substitution the code exists to prevent, and it had been true since the field was introduced. `a2a thread --json` and `a2a show --json` now both carry `reason_code` on the event that supplied one — `show` in particular, since that is the command you run to find out why something was refused.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing declines already carry the code in their committed events, so it becomes visible with no migration and no re-authoring.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-18",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Recording a triage verdict no longer rewrites the report it is judging",
          "detail": "`a2a feedback triage --apply` changed two fields and rewrote the whole record to do it: every top-level key was reordered alphabetically and every value re-quoted or re-folded. Nothing about the report changed and every byte did, so a reporter's own submitted text stopped being the text the corpus carried. That matters beyond tidiness, because the feedback corpus exists in two copies — where reports arrive and where verdicts are written — and the rule reconciling them is that a record's body is authored in the first and its status and resolution in the second. Reformatting made every record look like its body had changed too, so the reconciliation refused records nothing was wrong with. A verdict now rewrites the `status` line and replaces, or inserts, the `resolution` block, and leaves the rest of the file exactly as submitted. A resolution a YAML block scalar cannot carry without altering it — one ending in a newline, for instance — is now refused by name before anything is written, rather than being silently truncated. The observable effect on the public corpus is that a triage diff is now two fields wide instead of whole-file: a record's key order and the quoting and folding of every value it arrived with are preserved, so reading what a verdict changed no longer means reading the whole record.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a feedback triage --apply"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Records already triaged keep their current content; the change applies to verdicts recorded from here on.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-19",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "`a2a new` no longer picks an enum value you did not choose",
          "detail": "A template's pipe-alternatives placeholder — `\u003crelease|deprecation|…\u003e` — was filled with its FIRST alternative whenever you did not pass one. So a fresh announcement arrived as `category: release`, a fresh question as `category: clarification`, and, worst of the six, a fresh response as `result: answered` before anybody had answered anything. Nothing downstream could tell that from a choice you had made. The placeholder now survives rendering, and both validators refuse it: V1 `a2a validate` rejects it as a schema enum violation, and `a2a validate --ci` / `a2a submit` refuse it as an unfilled placeholder. Six of the eight artifact types are affected — announcement, contract, question, requirement and work_request carry `category`; response carries `result`. `decision` and `handoff` have no enum placeholder.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a new",
            "a2a validate",
            "a2a submit",
            "artifact templates"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Pass the value you mean. A draft you would previously have submitted without choosing is now refused until you do — which is the point, but it will refuse scripts that relied on the silent default.\n",
            "run": [
              "a2a new announcement --field category=notice",
              "a2a new response --field result=delivered \u003cparent-id\u003e"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-20",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`--field` stops accepting a value for a field it cannot actually set",
          "detail": "`a2a new --field \u003ckey\u003e=\u003cvalue\u003e` appends a single scalar for a key the template does not carry, so it correctly refuses array- and object-typed fields — it has no way to write them. That refusal was keyed on the schema's inline `type`, and read nothing at all for a field declared through a `$ref`. On an envelope/v2 contract, `generated_from` is exactly that: `--field generated_from=…` was accepted, a bare string was written into a slot the schema declares as an object with required sub-fields, and the draft failed later at validate — far from the command that had accepted it. Local `$ref`s now resolve, so the refusal fires where it always should have, at the surface you typed.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a new --field"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The accepted-then-invalid drafts this produced were already being rejected at validate; the refusal simply arrives at the point of entry now.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-21",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "A packed data directory passes `a2a validate --ci` instead of being refused by it",
          "detail": "`a2a data pack` writes a package whose README.md carries no frontmatter — it cannot, because the package's digest is computed over exactly those bytes and adding any would break verification for everyone who fetches it. `a2a validate --ci` then treated that README as an exchange artifact and rejected it with POL-002. The tool's own output failed the tool's own check, and a real integration pull request sat open because of it. Artifact discovery now recognises a data package's path shape and leaves its payload alone, the same way it already recognises a contract descriptor. The exemption is not a blanket skip of the system's section: a genuinely malformed artifact elsewhere under the same system is still refused.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate --ci",
            "a2a data pack",
            "space CI"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. A package that was previously blocked at CI will pass on the next run with no change to the package itself.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-22",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A closed, cancelled or superseded artifact explains itself instead of falling back to boilerplate",
          "detail": "Seventeen resting states — a cancelled question, a retired contract, an accepted handoff, a superseded anything — had no entry in the table that answers \"whose move is it\", and fell through to generic text that named the kind and state back at you and said nothing else. Each now carries a real reason: settled by the sender's own act; the sunset already ran; the successor carries its own pendency. `a2a inbox --json` and `a2a thread --json` report a different `why` for those artifacts as a result. The obligation is unchanged — nobody owed a move before and nobody owes one now; what changed is that the answer says why. Alongside it, one transition was removed: a disputed response could in principle be superseded, but no shipped command could ever reach that, and the real remedy already exists one level up — a dispute reopens the parent, where a fresh response is owed.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a inbox",
            "a2a thread",
            "the dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. No stored state changes; the same artifacts read back with a better explanation.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-23",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A response citing an acceptance criterion that does not exist is refused on every path, not some",
          "detail": "A partial response names which of the parent's acceptance criteria are unmet, by index. An index the parent does not have is refused — but only on two of the paths that submit one. The check was attached to individual call sites rather than to the thing they all build, so a path that constructed its own resolver silently skipped it, including the merge-time check a space pins in its own CI. Counting a parent's criteria is now a property of the resolver itself, held in place by the compiler, so a submit path gets the check by existing rather than by remembering.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a respond",
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a validate --ci",
            "contract and work write paths"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do — unless you have a draft with an out-of-range `unmet[]` index that previously slipped through one of those paths, which will now be refused with REF-018 and needs the index corrected.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-24",
          "kind": "policy",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "How long a feedback submit waits for a concurrent one is now a pinned decision",
          "detail": "Two `a2a feedback submit` runs on the same machine serialise through a lock on the local ledger. The loser waits two seconds and is then told to retry, rather than blocking indefinitely — a deliberate choice, because a human is usually watching and an actionable refusal beats an unbounded wait. That two seconds had never been asserted anywhere, so it was one edit away from becoming whatever a test found convenient. It is now pinned by its own check: raising it is a decision about how long an operator waits, and has to be made as one.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a feedback submit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The wait is unchanged at two seconds; what changed is that it can no longer drift silently.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-25",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "You are no longer told to close a delivery whose bytes are not in the space",
          "detail": "Once a question or work_request reached `responded`, the sender was told they owed a `close` — unconditionally, whether or not the delivery that response claims actually resolves. That produced the worst kind of wrong answer: a delivery's response merged while its payload pull request stayed open and red, the thread advanced to `verify:\u003cconsumer\u003e` for a package `a2a data fetch` correctly refuses as absent, and both ends reported success. The next move is now conditional on the bytes existing. When the package a delivery names cannot be resolved in the space, the move is named against the responder — who can still act — rather than the sender, who cannot verify what is not there. `a2a inbox --json`, `a2a thread --json` and the dashboard all read this from the same place, so they cannot disagree about it. The correlation runs through the handoff that carries the package, since that is the only artifact which structurally names one — and that is also the limit, stated here rather than discovered: this catches the case where the handoff arrived and its package did not. It does NOT catch the case where neither arrived, because `result: delivered` on its own claims no bytes — it is the ordinary result word for a work_request — so a space cannot tell \"delivered, bytes pending\" from \"delivered, no bytes were ever involved\" until a response can name its own package.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a inbox",
            "a2a thread",
            "the dashboard",
            "whose-move-is-it"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing threads re-read with the corrected next move; no stored state changes.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-26",
          "kind": "schema",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "An event that verifies or closes can record a verdict per acceptance criterion",
          "detail": "`event/v2` gains `verdicts[]` — for each of the parent's acceptance criteria, by index, what the verdict was and who caused it. It uses the same index grammar an incomplete response already uses for `unmet[]`, and the same range check, so the two cannot drift apart. An index that does not resolve in the parent is refused as `REF-019`. Read the limits before relying on it, because they are real: the two transitions this is required on — verify and close — are still written as `event/v1` by the current binary, so nothing it writes today carries the field; the range check has no wired call site yet; and while the field is decoded, no surface renders it. It is a wire contract you can author against and a validator that is proven reachable — not yet a working feature end to end.\n",
          "affects": [
            "event/v2",
            "a2a verify",
            "a2a close"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The field is optional on every event the binary currently produces.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-27",
          "kind": "break",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "`a2a feedback triage` checks the hub before it tells you the inbox is clean",
          "detail": "Triage read only the local tree. So it printed `inbox clean` while reports sat unread on the hub the tool itself submits to — which is exactly what happened: three of them, for up to three days, one of them blocking a live delivery. Run from a git work tree, triage now resolves the hub of record first and REFUSES rather than reporting, naming both the drift and the hub, when the hub carries a report this tree does not have, when the comparison cannot be completed at all, or when no hub is configured. `inbox clean` is reachable only after the hub is confirmed current — or when the directory is not a git work tree, where there is no hub to diverge from. Read the cost before you upgrade: **bare `triage` from a work tree now needs the network**, and refuses without it. That is deliberate — an unchecked \"clean\" is the failure this replaces — but it is a change in what an offline invocation does. `a2a feedback triage --apply` and every other feedback verb are unaffected; only the listing form checks.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a feedback triage"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "If you triage offline, expect a refusal that names the reason instead of a listing. Run `make feedback-sync` (or work from a non-work-tree copy) when you need the queue without reaching the hub.\n",
            "run": [
              "make feedback-sync"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-28",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The dashboard shows what a work request attached, and `a2a show` stops deriving it separately",
          "detail": "A work request can declare attachments — bytes it references by digest, with a role, a verification claim and a retention window. Until now only `a2a show` rendered them, from its own decode of the raw frontmatter, and the dashboard showed nothing at all. The claim is now derived once, where the artifact is read, and every surface reads the same value: `a2a show`, `a2a show --json` (a new `attachments` key, present only when there are any), the MCP show tool, and the dashboard's artifact page. That also fixes a rendering that could not happen. A lapsed retention was supposed to name its date instead of leaking an internal error — the code for it existed, but the field it needs, `expires_at`, was never read back off a committed artifact, so the case was unreachable outside a hand-built test. It reaches a real draft now, and the lapse is judged against the reader's own clock.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a show",
            "a2a show --json",
            "the dashboard",
            "a2a_show (MCP)"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The JSON key is additive and omitted when an artifact has no attachments, so an existing consumer sees no change.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-29",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a new work_request` writes the generation `a2a attach` requires",
          "detail": "`a2a attach` carries bytes with a work request — the case the tool was built for. It refuses any draft that is not an `envelope/v2` work_request, because `attachments` exists only on that generation. And `a2a new work_request` wrote `envelope/v1`. So attaching bytes to a drafted request meant hand-editing the `schema:` line first, and the refusal's own advice — \"run `a2a new work_request`, which authors envelope/v2 by default\" — was not true. It is now. `a2a new work_request` writes `envelope/v2`, and `a2a template show work_request` shows the same document, from the same decision, so the template an agent reads and the draft the tool renders cannot disagree. A v2 draft is a strict superset of a v1 one — the two fields it adds, `attachments` and `binding`, are both optional — so nothing an author could write before stops being writable. `binding` (what an artifact IS, and whether it may be adopted or pinned) is offered as commented guidance rather than a filled-in block, deliberately: leaving it out means \"undeclared\", which is a different statement from declaring no binding at all, and the tool should not make that statement on an author's behalf. To declare it in one flag: `a2a new work_request --field binding=none`. Other artifact types are unchanged. `announcement` in particular keeps writing `envelope/v1`: its v2 template describes a work checkpoint, not a general announcement, so switching it would render the wrong document.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a new work_request",
            "a2a template show work_request",
            "a2a attach"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing v1 work requests stay valid and are still accepted everywhere; only what a FRESH draft declares has changed.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-30",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A response cannot claim a delivery whose bytes the space does not have",
          "detail": "When a work request is completed by a body of work rather than an answer, the response points at the handoff, and the handoff lists what was delivered. Until now nothing checked that the listed data packages actually resolve — so a response could merge saying `delivered` while the package it stood for was still an open pull request, and both ends reported success. Submitting such a response is now refused, naming the handoff and the deliverable that does not resolve. The check reads the space's own committed history, never the author's working copy, so \"it resolves on my machine\" cannot pass it. It is narrow on purpose. `delivered` is the ordinary word for finishing a request, and most responses that use it involve no data package at all — those are untouched. The refusal applies only to a response that references a handoff which itself declares a data deliverable. `a2a respond` gained `--ref \u003cartifact-id\u003e` (repeatable) to carry that reference: the envelope always had a `refs` field, and no lifecycle verb could write it, because `--field` cannot fill a list. Known gap, deliberate: the MCP respond tool has no equivalent input yet, so an MCP-authored response cannot carry refs. The refusal itself is in the shared write path, so nothing is left unchecked.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a respond",
            "a2a respond --ref",
            "submitting a response"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do unless you author a response that references a handoff with a data deliverable. If you do and it is refused, the package has not landed yet — merge it first, then resubmit.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-31",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A contract can declare itself non-adoptable, and adoption refuses it",
          "detail": "Some contracts exist to be read, not adopted: a review bundle, an agreed definition with no machine-checkable grammar. Publishing one was impossible — every contract must carry a schema plus valid and invalid fixtures, and these have nothing to put there — so producers either faked a schema or sent the thing outside the protocol. Contracts gain an optional `x_binding` declaration: either the single word `none`, or four fields saying what the artefact is (`artifact_class`), what compatibility it claims (`compatibility_status`), and whether it may be adopted or pinned against. Leaving it out means undeclared, which is deliberately different from declaring `none`. Two things follow. `a2a contract adopt` refuses a contract that declares itself non-adoptable, before it resolves `--major`, so the flag cannot route around it — and since adopt is what writes the dependency pin, refusing it is what stops the pin. And a contract that claims no compatibility at all may publish without schemas or fixtures, because there is nothing to check. The relaxation is narrower than the refusal on purpose: a contract may be unadoptable while still naming a real compatibility claim, and that claim still needs its baseline. Known limit, stated rather than implied: no surface displays these fields yet, so a consumer learns a contract is unadoptable by being refused at adopt rather than by reading it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract adopt",
            "a2a contract publish",
            "a2a validate --ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The field is optional and absent from every existing contract; a contract that does not declare it behaves exactly as before.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-32",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a verify` records a verdict per acceptance criterion",
          "detail": "Verifying a response was an all-or-nothing act: the judgement of each individual acceptance criterion lived in prose, if anywhere, so a reader could not tell which promises were met, which were not warranted, and which were simply never exercised. `a2a verify` now takes `--verdict \u003cindex\u003e:\u003cverdict\u003e:\u003ccause_owner\u003e`, repeatable — one entry per criterion, indexed into the parent's own `acceptance_criteria` list, with `met`, `unmet`, `not_warranted` or `not_exercised` and the party the shortfall belongs to. The verdicts are written onto the verification event itself and shown per criterion in the thread and on the dashboard. An index that does not exist on the parent is refused at merge, so a verdict cannot quietly point at nothing. This applies once your space's `min_binary_version` has reached the contract-publication floor; below it, verification records exactly as it did before and the flag is refused rather than silently dropped. Known limit: the standalone `a2a close` verb does not yet carry verdicts — only verification does, including the same-PR close it performs for you.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a verify",
            "a2a verify --verdict",
            "a2a thread",
            "the dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The flag is optional; a verification without it records an empty verdict list and behaves as before.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-33",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A published contract with a registered consumer names who owes the operational half",
          "detail": "Publishing an interface is not the same as making it usable. An endpoint has to exist, credentials have to be issued, a registration has to happen — and none of that was anywhere in the record, so a consumer could adopt a contract, build a client, and wait indefinitely while every surface reported that nothing was outstanding. A published contract that has at least one registered consumer now produces an owed move on the producer, shown wherever open items are. This does NOT require the producer to have declared anything: the obligation is derived from facts the space already holds — the contract is published, and somebody adopted it. An operational half nobody has spoken about is exactly the case worth surfacing. Producers can be specific. A contract may declare `x_operational` — named items such as an endpoint or a credential channel, each `ready` or `absent`, each with an optional target date. Leaving it out means undeclared, which is a different statement from declaring the parts are absent, and the tool never fills it in for you. `a2a contract activate --version \u003cv\u003e --satisfies \u003citem\u003e` is how a producer records that a named item now exists. It refuses to activate an item the contract never declared, a version that was never published, or a contract belonging to another system. This applies once your space's `min_binary_version` has reached the contract-publication floor; below it no derived row appears and threads read exactly as they did.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract activate",
            "a2a inbox",
            "a2a thread",
            "the dashboard",
            "a2a_contract (MCP)"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Existing contracts gain no obligation until they have a registered consumer, and the declaration is optional.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-34",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "MCP now refuses to adopt a contract that declares itself non-adoptable",
          "detail": "A contract can declare that it must not be adopted, and adopting one is what pins a dependency against it. `a2a contract adopt` refused such a contract from the moment the declaration shipped. The MCP tool did not: the same action wrote the same dependency entry and never looked at the declaration, so an agent working over MCP could pin what an agent at the command line could not. Both surfaces refuse now, with the same message. The check also runs before the version is resolved, so supplying an explicit major no longer skips reading the contract's own declaration.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a_contract (MCP)"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If an MCP-driven adoption starts being refused, the contract was always declaring itself non-adoptable and the refusal was missing.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-35",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A body that hand-writes a field as `key = value` prose is refused",
          "detail": "When a field an agent needs does not exist, the agent writes it into the body anyway — a fenced block of `key = value` lines that reads as machine-readable and is not. It happened on a real request, and nothing caught it: the declaration sat in prose while every surface reported the artifact carried no such claim. Submitting or validating an artifact whose body hand-writes a line naming a field that IS declared on that artifact's own schema is now refused, naming the key and telling the author to declare it where it belongs. The check derives its list from the artifact's own type, so a field added to the schema tomorrow is covered the same day — nothing to keep in step by hand. It is line-anchored, so ordinary prose mentioning something like `x = y` mid-sentence does not trip it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate",
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a validate --ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "If a draft is refused, the body is declaring something the schema has a real field for — move it into the frontmatter. A body that refers to a field name in ordinary prose is unaffected.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-36",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a close` records per-criterion verdicts, like the close inside `a2a verify`",
          "detail": "Verifying a response can close its parent in the same pull request, and that close records a verdict for each acceptance criterion. Closing the same artifact directly with `a2a close` did not — it wrote the older event shape with no verdicts at all. One act, two records, depending on the route. `a2a close` now takes the same repeatable `--verdict \u003cindex\u003e:\u003cverdict\u003e:\u003ccause_owner\u003e` flag and writes the same event shape, once your space's floor allows it; below that floor it records exactly as before. No other lifecycle verb gained the flag — passing it to one is refused.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a close",
            "a2a close --verdict"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The flag is optional and a close without it records an empty verdict list.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-37",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a inbox` and `a2a outbox` show a contract's operational readiness",
          "detail": "A contract can declare the operational items an integration needs — an endpoint, a credential channel, a registration — each marked ready or absent. Leaving an item out is meaningful too: it means nobody has said anything about it, which is not the same as saying it is missing. `a2a inbox --json` and `a2a outbox --json` now carry that per-item readiness for contracts, as `operational_items`, alongside the artifact page and thread view that already showed it. All five read the same derived answer, so no surface can disagree with another about what a contract has declared. Only contracts carry these rows; other artifact kinds are unaffected.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a inbox --json",
            "a2a outbox --json"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The key is additive and omitted for artifacts that are not contracts.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-38",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a new --field` reaches a field that only the newer envelope has",
          "detail": "Drafting an artifact and setting one of the newer envelope fields in the same command did not work: `a2a new work_request --field binding=none` and `a2a new contract --field x_binding=none` were both refused with \"the envelope/v1 schema has no such key\" — even though those fields exist on exactly the generation those types are drafted at. Drafting the artifact and then editing the file by hand was the only route. Both work now. The refusal came from an internal first pass that reads the draft to decide which generation to render; it was applying your overrides to that pass, and judging them against the wrong generation. An override the renderer genuinely cannot apply is still refused, and still refused at the point you gave it. Separately: a binary built from source without a release version stamp could not submit at all, and said only `prepared-floor-stale`. It now names what it saw and how to build a binary that can write.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a new --field",
            "a2a submit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Both were refusals of things that should have worked.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-39",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The dashboard shows the operational catalogue, per-criterion verdicts, and what a contract refuses",
          "detail": "`a2a html` and `a2a serve` carried several of this release's new facts in their data and painted none of them. The sharpest case was the one this release is named for: a contract's operational catalogue distinguishes an item declared MISSING from one nobody ever mentioned, and on the page those two read identically — which is to say the distinction was not there at all for anyone reading it. Three states now read distinctly, and the page says outright that never mentioned is not the same claim as declared missing: one is a gap you can plan around and ask about, the other is silence. Four more facts arrived with it. The whose-move panel shows why a move is owed, which move it expects, and — where one applies — that the move belongs to a person, so a surface can no longer name a move the tool would then refuse. An `activate` event finally has a name in the timeline instead of falling through as a neutral informational row, in both languages. Verify and close events show their per-criterion verdicts on the artifact page, not only in the thread. Attachments show their content digest. And a contract that declares itself non-adoptable says so on its own card, before `a2a contract adopt` refuses. A test now requires each of these fields to reach the shipped page, so the class of defect behind all of them — a field that is computed, typed, passed along, and read by nothing — cannot merge again unnoticed.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html",
            "a2a serve"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Open the dashboard; the new sections appear wherever the underlying artifact carries them.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-40",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a attach` can actually send, and `a2a fetch` reads any attachment back",
          "detail": "An attachment's `ref` was documented as a content-addressed blob id and there was no blob. `a2a attach` wrote a bare digest, the space resolved references only as data-package ids, and so every draft the verb produced was refused at submit — the only way to make a reference resolvable was `a2a data pack --contract`, pinning a contract that an ad hoc bundle does not conform to. There is a blob now. `a2a attach` mints a `BL-` id, writes the bytes into the space, and records the id in the draft; the draft submits. `a2a fetch \u003cBL-id\u003e --to \u003cdir\u003e` reads any attachment back, digest-verified, whether or not it was ever contract-pinned. `a2a data pack`/`deliver` are unchanged and remain the contract-pinned specialisation for a counterparty delivering a conforming payload back to you. Three ceilings bound what a blob may weigh, and each refusal names which ceiling it hit and its value — a git-backed space should not carry large blobs, and now it says so rather than trying.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a attach",
            "a2a fetch",
            "a2a submit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "`a2a attach` is now a NETWORK WRITE. It needs `a2a init`, a connected space and a credential, exactly like `a2a data deliver` — it is no longer a local edit to a draft file, and it will fail differently when you are offline. That ordering is what makes the submit-time check meaningful: the bytes are proven fetchable from the space's perspective, not from the author's laptop.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-41",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Contract refusals name what is wrong and what to do, on both surfaces",
          "detail": "`a2a contract activate`, `deprecate` and `retire` refused a contract the local mirror does not carry by printing the raw file-open failure with an absolute cache path. They now say what happened and what to do — run `a2a sync`, or check the contract has been published. The same messages had already been fixed on one surface and not the other, so the identical condition produced a clear refusal for a person and a raw path for an agent. Both surfaces now say the same thing, and a test compares them, so the two cannot drift apart again silently. Separately, a submit that cannot reach the host no longer hides a local problem behind a network one: a response naming a delivery the space cannot resolve now says so, instead of surfacing a connection error the author cannot act on. An attachment already behaved this way; the two kinds of unresolvable reference now behave alike.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract activate",
            "a2a contract deprecate",
            "a2a contract retire",
            "a2a submit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Same conditions refuse, with messages you can act on.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-42",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "A supersession fork or cycle is now refused instead of merging clean",
          "detail": "Two successors claiming one predecessor, or a chain that loops back on itself, used to pass validation. Both are now refused by the full-repo pass with their own codes — REF-020 for a fork, REF-021 for a cycle, self-supersede included. A cycle is the one that matters most: an artifact reachable from itself through a supersedes chain has no state a reader can settle on, so the same history could fold differently depending on where you started. The refusal names both claimants rather than \"the second one\", because in a whole-repository walk there is no stable notion of which arrived second. The check runs only in the full-repository mode, and deliberately: a pull request carries a slice of the graph, and a partial graph would report a fork that does not exist.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate --ci --mode=v3-full-repo"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "Nothing to do for a healthy space. A space whose history already carries a fork or a cycle will see its full-repo validation red with the offending ids named; that history was already ambiguous and the refusal is what makes it visible.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-43",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a attach --json` now reports the branch and pull request it opened",
          "detail": "The JSON result carried the draft and the attachment and dropped the write result its own internal value already held. The branch and pull request appeared only in the plain-text output, so a script or an MCP client could not follow the write it had just made. It now carries the same write result the `data` verbs expose, in the same shape, so one spelling of the fact serves both.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a attach --json"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The field is added; nothing that existed changed shape.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-44",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a data pack` no longer calls an unsynced mirror an unpublished contract",
          "detail": "Packing against a contract version your checkout had not synced yet failed with \"contract version is not published\" — a sentence that blames the contract's author for the reader's own stale copy. The two states are different and only one of them is anybody's fault. The resolver now says which it is. It claims \"never published\" only on positive evidence — the mirror's own descriptor already at or past the requested version with no publish event naming it — and otherwise tells you the version is not in your local mirror and to run `a2a sync`. Defaulting to the actionable answer is deliberate: absence in a local copy is not evidence of absence in the space.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a data pack"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "If you have scripted against the old sentence, the unsynced case now emits a different one. Run `a2a sync` when you see it.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-45",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a doctor`'s documented check list matches what it prints",
          "detail": "The troubleshooting page said `doctor` runs sixteen checks and listed seventeen rows, promised they appear \"in the order the binary prints them\" while placing `repository visibility` eighth when it is printed last, and said each check runs once per connected space — which is backwards for sixteen of the seventeen. Only `repository visibility` repeats per space; the rest fold their per-space detail into one line. An agent counting or parsing doctor's output was off by at least one row, and by one MORE for every additional connected space. The page now states the fixed sixteen and the seventeenth separately, says which repeats and which does not, and lists the table in output order. What let it drift is the part worth reporting: a test already asserted every printed check has a documented row, and it passed, because it ran doctor with no connected space configured — so the seventeenth line was never emitted and the documented count was true inside the fixture. The fixture now configures a space, and asserts the visibility row was actually seen rather than counting whatever appeared.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you parse `a2a doctor`'s output, note that the `repository visibility` rows come last and there is one per connected space.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-46",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The operating skill teaches this release, and is reorganised around finding one thing",
          "detail": "The skill shipped inside the binary describes what this release actually does. Everything below was missing, and each is written with the reading an agent would otherwise get wrong, not merely named. The derived state fields on every read surface — `outcome`, `terminal`, `state_since`, `state_by`, `state_event`, and `transition_free` on events. `refused` does not mean nobody owes anything: a rejected handoff is refused AND non-terminal, and the producer owes the resubmission. `terminal` is a third question, not the negation of the first. The state clock is not the activity clock. And a missing field is not a default — an artifact whose state no event produced carries none of them. A cluster nothing had ever documented: how far you may trust the copy in front of you. `sync_stale` is a fail-safe, not a measurement — it is also true when the mirror was never fetched. `sync_age: 0s` means never synced, not just synced. `pending_merge` is a statement about your machine, and its absence is weaker than it looks. `new` is a shared cursor that an MCP read consumes for the CLI. Beside them, the receipt fields that say where a record's CLAIM and the space's own answer disagree. Then the capabilities that had shipped with no page: `a2a feedback triage` refusing offline, a response that cannot claim a delivery whose bytes the space lacks, multi-addressee action, a handoff superseded after its receiver left, `attempted`/`residue`, the fork-and-cycle refusal on supersession, `a2a contract check`, `a2a await`, `a2a serve`, `a2a whatsnew`, and `a2a disconnect` — which is local only, and does not offboard you from anything. The corpus is also reorganised. `loops.md` was one 64 KB page holding thirteen jobs; it is now a short root with a table that routes you by the SITUATION you are in rather than by the loop's name, and seven loop pages behind it. The data-exchange reference splits into its flags and its refusals. Every page opens with the same three lines: what it answers, when you need it, what it does not cover. One correction: the receive loop told agents to supersede a disputed response. That transition does not exist — it was removed before this release — and the real remedy was in the same sentence all along: a dispute reopens the parent, where a fresh response is owed. Two routing repairs, both found by handing an agent the installed tree and ten real tasks. \"I owe them the export\" used to route only to the contract-pinned `a2a data` path, while `a2a attach` — the answer when the bytes simply belong with the artifact you are drafting — appeared in no index at all; both paths are now named with the test that separates them (if no contract version pins the shape of the bytes, you want `attach`). And `attach`'s three optional flags now say what each DECIDES: `--role` is a free-text label, `--conforms-to` writes a CLAIM and checks nothing, and `--retention` defaults to one week rather than forever, so an attachment nobody thought about lapses after a review round.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a skill install",
            "the published documentation"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Run `a2a skill install` to refresh the copy in your repo. Page filenames under the skill changed, so a bookmark or a hard-coded path into `loops.md` needs updating; `loops.md` itself still exists and routes to everything.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-47",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "`a2a __catalog --surfaces --json` publishes what each read surface emits",
          "detail": "The hidden `__catalog` verb gained a second machine-readable mode: every JSON key each agent-facing read surface emits, per surface, derived by reflecting over the types the binary serialises. It exists because the obvious alternative is provably blind. Most of these fields are `omitempty` and one is `omitzero`, and they populate only from the event that produced a state — so a fixture whose artifacts have no such event emits none of them, and a gate that learned its universe by running the binary would have declared that universe empty and gone green. Asking the types instead makes it complete by construction. Its consumer is a new gate that holds every command, MCP tool and derived read-surface field to being taught in the skill or structurally declared. That gate is why the documentation above exists rather than being noticed a release later. What the key list means, for anyone parsing it: the walker unwraps pointers, slices, arrays and maps to the element type and inlines embedded structs, so a key appears once per SURFACE regardless of how deeply the shape nests it, and `json:\"-\"` fields never appear at all. It is the set of names that can reach the wire, not a per-response inventory — a field that is `omitempty` and unset on every artifact you happen to hold is still in this list, which is the entire point.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. `__catalog` is hidden and machine-consumed; no agent or operator is meant to call it directly.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-48",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The demo dashboard reports what the domain says a state means",
          "detail": "`a2a html --demo` renders a hand-maintained fixture whose job is to cover every type and state the dashboard can draw. It predates this release's own headline fields, and `terminal` has no `omitempty` — so the demo asserted `terminal: false` on all 31 of its artifacts, including a `closed` question and a `cancelled` work request, both of which the domain calls terminal. Absent would have been a gap; `false` was a wrong answer, in the showcase for the release that added the right one. The fixture now derives them. Outcome and terminality are asked of the same `fold` functions the live dashboard calls for a real artifact, so a demo row cannot disagree with the product about its own state. The three state-provenance fields — since when, by whom, which event — are read off the fixture's own events, skipping any event the fixture flags, because an illegal or unauthorized transition changes nothing and must not claim authorship of a state it never moved. Where the fixture states no state-moving event, all three stay absent rather than being invented, which is the same answer the real read model gives. Visibly, one artifact detail changes its verdict: a cancelled work request read \"Document final · no protocol move is owed\" and now reads \"Document cancelled\". Finished and abandoned are different facts, and telling them apart is what this release is for. The public dashboard example on a2ahub.dev had a second copy of the same problem, from the other direction: the site build read the RAW fixture while the local dashboard rendered the derived model, so one fixture produced two answers and the site knew less than the binary did. The site now consumes the derived model directly rather than re-deriving anything in the browser — a browser deciding what a state means is the defect this release removes.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a html --demo",
            "a2a dashboard --demo",
            "a2ahub.dev dashboard example"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The demo fixture is synthetic and no connected space is involved; a real space was always read through the live path, which has carried these fields since they shipped.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01910-49",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "The roadmap's text projection stops contradicting the roadmap",
          "detail": "a2ahub.dev serves a Markdown projection of each page for agents and other machine readers. The roadmap's projection carried a hand-typed heading — \"Proposal — verified data transfer between agents and systems … it has no release target\" — while the page it projects had already moved that capability into Shipped and renamed the open proposal to the autonomous delivery loop. So one page said, in two places, that verified data transfer both shipped and had no release target, and the machine-readable half was the wrong one. The heading and its summary are now read from the same source the page renders, and a stale needle fails the build instead of silently keeping yesterday's wording.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2ahub.dev/roadmap.md"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you consumed `roadmap.md` and recorded that verified data transfer had no release target, that reading was wrong from v0.19.10's own content onward — the delivery-and-verdict loop ships in this release line.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.19.11",
      "released": "2026-08-12",
      "headline": "The site can publish a release again, and every page got lighter doing it",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-01911-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a2ahub.dev publishes the current release again",
          "detail": "v0.19.10 was tagged and its GitHub Release published, and the site build failed with forty-two pages over their size budgets — so a2ahub.dev kept serving v0.19.8. That tag push is the ONLY trigger that deploys the site on a release: the promotion commit touches the space template, which the deploy workflow's path filter excludes. The release shipped and the site did not hear about it, for the fifth time. The release being large was the trigger, not the cause. Two payloads no reader consumes were riding on every page and only crossed a line when a 49-note release arrived. The newest release's entire change list was injected into every page that shows a version number — 24 KiB gzipped — while all five consumers read only the version string. The demo payload carried three releases of full prose, 32 KiB gzipped, twice the weight of the whole rest of the demo; it now carries a bounded five entries per release, which is what a panel demonstrating its own shape needs, and says so in its own scope line. Both were deleted rather than budgeted for. Every page is now lighter than it was BEFORE v0.19.10: the front page 48 to 23 KiB, the docs index 54 to 30, features 57 to 33, the guided dashboard example 87 to 39, the changelog 65 to 41. No page budget was raised.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2ahub.dev"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you read a2ahub.dev between 2026-08-07 and this release, the changelog you saw ended at v0.19.8; v0.19.9 and v0.19.10 are both in it now.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01911-2",
          "kind": "policy",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "This release was verified as presentation-only, and no live GitHub run was made for it",
          "detail": "The release tier is chosen from the exact public diff, not from the version number. This one changes no executable or protocol behaviour: release notes, one Go test's corpus tripwire, the repository's own CI triggers, a repository gate script, the website build, and version pins whose workflow logic is unchanged. No file under `internal/`, `cmd/` or `schemas/` changed outside a test. So the real-GitHub matrix was NOT run for v0.19.11, and that is the tier's normal answer rather than a deferral: there is no provider behaviour in this diff for it to observe. What WAS run: the full `make check` ceiling including the offline logic tier, the website's own build and budget checks, the release preflight, and the publisher's leak assertion over the filtered candidate. The last full live GitHub matrix ran on 2026-08-05. Every release since — v0.19.6 through v0.19.10 — deferred the provider tier by explicit operator authorization, which `KI-01900-PROVIDER-TIER-DEFERRED` describes in full and which remains the honest measure of what is unproven. v0.19.11 adds nothing to that list, because it adds no behaviour.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. If you are auditing what this version proved, the two artefacts are this note and the standing known issue it points at.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-01911-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "Cutting a release stops running the same CI three times",
          "detail": "The product CI workflow listened to every push with no branch or tag filter. A release pushes the candidate branch, then main, then the tag — three pushes of one commit — so the full gate suite ran three times on the same code, twice repeating a verdict already given. Tags no longer trigger it; the release workflow owns the tag and has its own trigger. Candidate branches still do, deliberately: that run happens before promotion, which is the only moment its verdict can still stop a release. Separately, the public repository's publish-boundary backstop still listed a shared gate helper as private after it was published, and reddened the default branch a minute after the v0.19.10 tag. It was the fifth restatement of one list, and the fifth to go stale.\n",
          "affects": [
            "repository CI"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. No product behaviour changes; this is the repository's own automation.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.20.0",
      "released": "2026-08-12",
      "headline": "A rule that could redden a branch nobody may edit, and the command that never looked",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-02000-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "REF-017 no longer refuses a digest quoted in ordinary prose",
          "detail": "v0.19.11 added REF-017 at severity `reject`: an artifact body naming a `sha256:` token that no declared attachment carries. The rule read a digest appearing in ordinary PROSE as a claim to carry bytes. A `work_request` merged four days before the rule existed — green at submit, quoting a contract-set digest in a sentence explaining which version the counterparty had adopted — turned a space's post-merge audit permanently red. Committed artifacts are immutable and no verb retracts a closed exchange, so there was no in-protocol repair. Reported as fb-20260812-e6d189.\nTwo candidate fixes were considered and both are unimplementable as stated. A contract-set digest and an attachment digest are the SAME SHAPE (`sha256:` + hex), so nothing discriminates them; and no envelope schema records the version that created an artifact, so \"do not apply retroactively\" has nothing to key on.\nREF-017 now refuses on a CONJUNCTION instead: the body names an undeclared digest AND enumerates what looks like a file tree AND the envelope declares no attachment at all. That is the exact shape of the incident the rule was written for — a request that named a bundle by digest and listed its eight files while the bytes sat in a gitignored directory. Either signal alone now WARNS as POL-017. A bare digest creates no expectation of bytes: a receiver cannot fetch from a digest it was merely told about.\nA second false positive was found and closed while pinning the first: the file-tree heuristic accepted an all-digit \"extension\", so three lines of prose opening with version strings (`v0.19.11 shipped the rule.`) counted as a file listing. An extension must now contain a letter.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate",
            "a2a validate --ci",
            "the space post-merge audit"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "If your space's default branch went red on REF-017 against an already-merged artifact, bump the space's workflow pin to this release and re-run the audit; the branch goes green with no edit to any artifact. Nothing to do if you never saw the code.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02000-1",
          "kind": "policy",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "REF-017 is enforced at the write gate and returns no verdict in the post-merge audit",
          "detail": "Narrowing the rule fixed the reported case. It did not fix the class: any future reject-severity code could redden a branch whose content nobody is permitted to change.\nSo REF-017 is now scoped by mode. At `--mode=v3-pr` — the pull-request gate, where refusing still PREVENTS the merge — it is unchanged, and still `reject`. At `--mode=v3-full-repo` — the post-merge audit, where the artifact is already committed and refusing can only punish — it returns no verdict at all. POL-017 is deliberately NOT suppressed there: it is a warning, so the audit still surfaces the smell without refusing anything immutable.\nA permanently red audit is a broken instrument, not a strict one.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a validate --ci --mode=v3-full-repo"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The write gate is unchanged, so nothing that was refused before is accepted now at the point where it can still be fixed.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02000-2",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "a2a doctor reports when a space's default branch is failing its own audit",
          "detail": "`doctor` answered fourteen PASS on a space whose default branch had been failing its post-merge audit for four hours. It has checks that look adjacent — `CI presence`, `stuck green PRs` — and neither notices that the last push to main FAILED. The one command an operator runs to ask \"is my space healthy\" answered yes, and the red branch was found only because an unrelated task had someone reading workflow logs by hand.\nReported as fb-20260812-ee6dcd.\nThere is now a `default branch healthy` check. It reads the required check run on the space's default branch — the same selection the pull-request gate itself uses, so the two cannot drift — and FAILs when it concluded `failure`, `timed_out`, `cancelled` or `action_required`, naming the space, the branch, the run and its conclusion. `doctor` exits 1.\nEvery uncertain state is a PASS carrying an advisory, never a red: this build wiring no ref-status reader, no credential resolving, an unreadable host, no matching check run, or a run still in flight. A check that cried wolf on a network blip would be ignored inside a week, and this row's whole purpose is being believed.\n`doctor` now prints seventeen fixed checks rather than sixteen.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Run `a2a doctor`. If the new row FAILs, the branch every participant pulls from and merges onto is red — fix it before merging onto it, because every subsequent merge stacks onto an already-failing main.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02000-3",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a data package's own README no longer reads as an undecodable artifact",
          "detail": "`a2a data pack` writes a `README.md` into `\u003csystem\u003e/data/\u003cDP-id\u003e/` with no frontmatter — the only shape it may write it in, since its bytes are covered by the package digest and adding frontmatter would break verification for everyone who fetches it. The validator learned to exempt that path. Two other readers did not.\n`inbox` and `outbox` listed it on every run as `not-frontmatter-shaped`, and `doctor`'s repository-visibility check returned `UNVERIFIED: classification scan incomplete` — no verdict at all, on a check whose job is deciding whether a space's contents suit its repository's visibility — while its remediation line named an owner who could not act.\nThe path grammar is now stated once, in `internal/artifact`, and every reader consults it. The file is classified, not excused.\nReported as fb-20260812-d31acb and escalated as fb-20260812-f9cfac, which was right to escalate: the first record called it listing noise and set `minor`; the second showed the same file stopping a verdict from being reached at all. One cause, two surfaces, and the surface looked at first set the severity.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a inbox",
            "a2a outbox",
            "a2a doctor",
            "a2a sync"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. The next `a2a sync`/`a2a doctor` stops reporting the file, and the visibility check reaches a verdict again.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02000-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the random `directory not empty` test failure is fixed at its cause",
          "detail": "`make check` reddened at random with `TempDir RemoveAll cleanup: unlinkat …/origin.git: directory not empty` — no assertion failing, just teardown. It red the public repository's own CI on a pull request whose entire diff was one YAML file, and blocked it from merging.\n`git-receive-pack` runs `git maintenance run --auto --quiet --detach` inside the receiving repository after every push. The `--detach` is the whole problem: that process outlives the push, keeps the repository directory open, and races the test's own cleanup.\nThe fixture layer already disabled `gc.auto` and `maintenance.auto` for exactly this class of flake. Measured against git 2.55.0, those do not prevent the spawn — they only make the already-forked process decide to do nothing once it starts. The setting that prevents it is `receive.autogc`, it belongs to the repository being pushed INTO, and a pushing client's config cannot set it. It is now set on every bare fixture repository, with a structural gate over the tree so a new one cannot be added without it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "the test suite"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do; no shipped behaviour changes. Named because a non-deterministic red is a thing people work around rather than report.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.21.0",
      "released": "2026-08-13",
      "headline": "The letterbox comes off the door that gets replaced, and CI stops buying evidence it cannot read",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-0",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "feedback lands on a dedicated branch the publisher never touches",
          "detail": "Public `main` was two things at once. It was the feedback hub of record — the default `a2a feedback submit` target for every shipped binary, with status read back over raw.githubusercontent.com against that branch. It was also the branch the publisher force-pushes wholesale on every release.\nMeasured on 2026-08-12, not reasoned about: two open feedback pull requests were both MERGEABLE and BEHIND after a publish, both with auto-merge armed and neither firing; merging the first put the second back into BEHIND. A record submitted after that same publish merged in twenty-three seconds. The difference was entirely whether a release happened while the record was open.\nInbound reports now land on `feedback-hub`, an orphan branch of the same public repository carrying the corpus and nothing else. The publisher touches exactly three refs and has no reason to touch a fourth; branch protection makes sure it cannot by accident, and that protection was verified by attempting a real force-push and watching it refused.\nThe repository does not change, so no binary in the field breaks: only the branch moves. The corpus was migrated by COPY, so `main` keeps its records for the length of the rollover window, and rollback is repointing a constant.\n",
          "affects": [
            "feedback"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Nothing to run. Reports you filed before this release are still readable at their old address; a current binary files new ones on the hub branch automatically."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a blob payload is no longer mistaken for a malformed artifact",
          "detail": "`a2a validate --ci` already carved `\u003csystem\u003e/blobs/\u003cBL-id\u003e/…` out of artifact discovery. The mirror walk did not, so the same bytes read as a broken envelope on the read path while validating cleanly on the write path.\nBoth readers now ask ONE predicate, `space.BlobForPath`, rather than carrying a copy each. The exemption stays scoped to the blob's own directory grammar: a genuinely malformed artifact filed elsewhere under the same system still reaches discovery and still reds, and a negative control test pins that.\n",
          "affects": [
            "cli"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Update, then re-read: a blob payload that showed as UNVERIFIED will decode."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "`a2a doctor` links the failing check run it names",
          "detail": "GitHub's check-runs API returns `html_url`; the host adapter never decoded it, so a `default branch healthy` failure could name the space, the branch, the selected check run and its conclusion — good evidence, and no link to click.\nThe result type now carries the run URL and the doctor row prints it. When the provider omits the field the message degrades to exactly what it said before, never to an empty link.\n",
          "affects": [
            "cli"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action — corrected in the binary."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-3",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "CI charges what the diff can justify",
          "detail": "A ten-times-priced macOS runner ran on every push and every pull request in both repositories, for about fifty-two seconds of Swift work — 55.3% of the measured bill over a twelve-day window, against ZERO commits touching the companion it compiles. It is now reachable only from a diff that can change its verdict, from a tag, or from one weekly run that exists so a filter which silently never fires cannot look like a filter that correctly never fires.\nA push now buys the derived lane; the ceiling runs at the ship boundary — candidate refs, tags, a nightly schedule and manual dispatch. Pull requests keep the ceiling deliberately: moving them would remove validating proof from branches that accept external writes, and that is gated behind a merge-gate precondition this release does not claim.\nSuperseded runs now cancel. A sub-minute gate that billed a whole runner minute folds into the job the run already pays for. Pages stops running in the repository where Pages is not enabled, where it had failed thirty times out of thirty-one.\nEvery decision lives in a script with its own self-tests, not in a workflow expression: the linter already in the pipeline was measured saying nothing at all about a job with no path condition and nothing about a missing timeout.\n",
          "affects": [
            "ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A maintainer-side change; nothing to run. A space's own CI is untouched."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-4",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the release-evidence gate counts releases, and the spend is a command",
          "detail": "The provider-tier gate counted deferral RECORDS, so a release that simply omitted the file was invisible to the mechanism built to make the streak refusable — and omitting it was easier than writing it. Re-keyed to releases, it found three releases that had shipped with neither a deferral record nor live-e2e evidence where one was known.\nThe CI spend is now re-derivable by one command, replayed offline against a persisted raw join rather than re-fetched. Its first act was to falsify two claims this project had written down about its own billing the day before.\nRe-keying that gate also exposed a defect in the direction nobody was watching. It ships to the published projection, but the evidence it judges is stripped there — so while it counted records it reported a confident \"0 outstanding\" about a streak it could not see, and counting releases would have turned that same blindness into a failure on every published candidate. It now declines when its inputs are absent, and says which ones.\n",
          "affects": [
            "ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A maintainer-side gate; nothing to run."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02100-5",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a new gate refuses the next ungated expensive runner",
          "detail": "Every job in every workflow must declare a timeout, sized at or above 1.5x its measured p99 from a committed artefact and under a sixty-minute cap, and a job on a non-ubuntu runner must be reachable only through a condition on what the diff touched.\nIt caught a real under-scoped timeout on its first run against the tree it was written for.\n",
          "affects": [
            "ci"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "A maintainer-side gate; nothing to run."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.22.0",
      "released": "2026-08-13",
      "headline": "A second data transport becomes a driver rather than a rewrite, and a lane stops running tests inside a clone of this repository",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-02200-0",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the transport-driver seam exists, with a conformance suite every driver proves itself through",
          "detail": "`internal/datatransport` ships the Go half of AC-8: a narrow `Driver` interface (its name, its locator grammar, moving bytes), a constructible registry that refuses a duplicate name and an unknown lookup, and an EXPORTED conformance suite so a driver's own package proves itself through the same one rather than writing its own.\nThe suite takes a harness rather than a bare driver, and that is the part worth knowing. It used to assert that a read immediately after a write returns the same bytes — a property of an in-memory store, not of a transport. The shipped transport writes through a pull request and reads the merged branch; every plausible second one (object storage, deletable release assets) has some publish boundary too. So each driver supplies how its bytes become readable, and byte-identity keeps its full strength instead of being weakened to the weakest transport.\nNothing imports the package yet, and the wire half is deliberately not here: `data-package/v1`'s `transport_driver` is a closed enum in a schema frozen by the published-v1 ratchet, so the enum opens in a v2 minted together with a real second driver's requirements rather than ahead of them.\n",
          "affects": [
            "contracts"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. No command, schema or lifecycle changed; this is a seam for a transport that does not exist yet."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02200-1",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the derived lane stopped selecting Go tests inside a cached clone of this repository",
          "detail": "The lane's `doc.go` walk pruned `.git` and `node_modules` and nothing else, so it found the cached clone's own package documentation and derived a real scoped-test phase pointing inside it. `make lane-run` then ran `go test` in a clone and reported a setup failure against a tree whose own packages were green.\nThe trigger was incidental and the exposure was broad: the bogus phase carried the cached package's globs unprefixed, so any local lane whose diff touched one of them selected it. CI never saw it — the phase set is derived once, before the run, and the cache is created during it — which means local and CI were answering slightly different questions.\n",
          "affects": [
            "cli"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action — a local gate stopped producing a false red."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02200-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "high",
          "subject": "a flaky module read no longer reddens a space's required merge check",
          "detail": "`a2a-validate-reusable.yml` ran `go run \u003cmodule\u003e@\u003cver\u003e`, which fused two failures under one exit code: \"the supply chain could not be read\" and \"this space is invalid\". Observed 2026-08-13 in a real space — a `sum.golang.org` tile read failed mid-graph and the required check went red; the same module downloaded cleanly on a retry.\nBuild and run are now separate, and only the build is retried, three times with a growing pause. A genuinely invalid space still reds once, never three times, because the validator runs outside the loop. When all three attempts fail the error says plainly that this is the module supply chain and not the space's content.\nThe integrity mechanism is unchanged: `go install` verifies through the Go checksum DB exactly as `go run` did, so this still costs no fetch token and no cosign step.\n",
          "affects": [
            "space"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Run `a2a space update` to pick it up. A space inherits this by pinning the workflow tag; no hand-edit."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "version": "0.23.0",
      "released": "2026-08-13",
      "headline": "The transport registry gets a real caller, and the dashboard starts explaining whose move it is",
      "changes": [
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-0",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a data package's declared transport_driver now selects the driver that moves its bytes",
          "detail": "`transport_driver` was written at exactly one site and read by nobody. `DeliverDataPackage` now decodes that one field and looks the driver up in the registry, and `internal/space` registers its own space-git driver lazily. A manifest naming an unregistered driver is refused by name.\nRegistration is a `sync.OnceValue` rather than an `init`: this repository bans init functions, and the ban is right — a registry populated at import time is precisely the implicit ordering with invisible side effects that rule exists to prevent.\nFound by the phase's own audit, which was right to call the promise overstated. The read half stays unwired and is stated rather than claimed: `a2a data fetch` still resolves directly, and a second driver would need a read hook that lands with `data-package/v2` — the wire enum is frozen in v1 regardless.\n",
          "affects": [
            "contracts"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. No command, flag or lifecycle changed; a manifest that was already valid still delivers exactly as before."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-1",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Overview attention cards say whose turn it is, what move is expected, and why",
          "detail": "Attention cards now carry the pendency verdict the domain already computed: waiting systems, expected transition and any human gate. A bilingual sentence explains the active reason in the card body; the rule citation remains available under Technical detail instead of being presented as the explanation.\nThe reason codes and pendency-row identity are typed and enumerated from their domain homes. Inbox and outbox JSON gain `human_gate` only when a gate exists; existing fields keep their values and empty rows keep the key omitted. The browser no longer infers this gate from a coincidental reason string, and the vocabulary gate now refuses the equivalent inline state-classification chain as well as a hand-built list.\nThis is the first slice of the dashboard-coherence work, not a claim that every dashboard family has already been rebuilt. The existing 4 MiB shell ceiling remains in force; measured headroom requires the next mapping phase to add honest item-family admission bounds.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard",
            "a2a inbox",
            "a2a outbox",
            "MCP"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Regenerate a dashboard with the current binary to see the clearer attention cards; existing JSON consumers may ignore the additive human_gate key."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-2",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard rows, threads and prompts now share one attention verdict",
          "detail": "Cache now builds the dashboard exchange and thread projections from one folded snapshot and evaluates each artifact's pendency verdict once. Missing or cross-thread response parents take one explicit degraded fallback, so the carried owner agrees with the matching legal action.\nOverdue and activation-needed status are carried as semantic booleans; the browser no longer recovers them by matching raw reason-code strings. The vocabulary gate also distinguishes a carried outcome operand from a state operand and refuses unknown state comparisons even when only one known state appears beside the typo.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Existing inbox/outbox JSON remains compatible; regenerate the dashboard to consume the corrected projection."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-3",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard attention order and Overview KPI membership now have one domain owner",
          "detail": "Cache now returns the complete six-tier attention order and the exact object sets behind Need you, No human move, You await and Lines off current. Counts are the lengths of those sets, so a tile and the list it opens cannot describe different objects. Dependency reads are bounded, strict and degradation-aware instead of silently treating a partial scan as a complete total.\nThe hidden binary vocabulary catalogue also adds the typed reason, pendency-rule and human-gate families without changing its existing outcome, state, transition or human-gate fields. The dashboard mapping phase consumes these answers next; this release entry does not claim the old browser formulas have already been removed.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. These are additive read-model and catalogue contracts; existing command output remains compatible."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-4",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard views now have independent component and lifecycle boundaries",
          "detail": "The local dashboard now composes one shell, nine view components, one root modal engine and one live-transport component. The root keeps normalized data, shared UI state, hydration and navigation; each view owns its own presentation mapping. Component discovery and the public Guide projection derive from the root import graph instead of a second registry.\nRendered behavior is unchanged: the generated embedded template matches the reviewed sources, the public site builds, and the existing browser contract passes across desktop/mobile, light/dark and Russian/English.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Regenerate the dashboard with the current binary; routes, data and visible behavior remain compatible."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-5",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "served dashboards expose one exact digest-coherent JSON view",
          "detail": "The loopback server now serves the exact compact JSON bytes embedded in the dashboard shell at /api/v1/dashboard. Conditional requests, SSE invalidations and retained generations share the exact view-model digest, while the semantic generation key also observes non-operational content. Root, snapshot and dashboard responses share one bounded writer pool and retain the last good generation when rendering or size validation fails.\nThe separate `a2a html --json` command remains byte-compatible: its existing two-space-indented output and trailing newline are unchanged.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard",
            "serve"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Existing dashboard URLs and CLI JSON output remain compatible; live clients may adopt the additive dashboard route."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-6",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard payloads carry one bounded copy of every domain answer",
          "detail": "Dashboard assembly now reads one folded snapshot and one dependency projection, maps each active item once, and reuses those objects for attention sets, thread open items and detail admission. Rule identity, the server-composed reason sentence, operational readiness, source synchronization, dependency degradations and document byte facts no longer fall through mapper gaps.\nEvery repeated root and nested collection now carries shown, total and truncated facts from the same stable prefix that produced it. Need-you objects win scarce detail slots, and the largest saturated fixture stays below the existing 2 MiB DATA and 4 MiB shell ceilings. The later view phases consume these additive fields; this entry does not claim those screen rewrites are already complete.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard",
            "a2a html --json",
            "serve"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Existing JSON fields keep their values; consumers may ignore the additive facts and windows until they adopt the rebuilt views."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-7",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard status words now come from one complete RU/EN vocabulary",
          "detail": "The binary catalogue preserves its established fields and adds owner-derived freshness, reason, rule, work-mode, dependency, consistency, operational and live-transport sets. One 71-entry dashboard dictionary covers the exact eleven-family universe, with complete RU and EN labels and explanations, six closed tones, six non-colour cues and a separate fallback that never echoes unknown input.\nThe browser has one resolver and one calm-policy seam. Calm treatment changes only chroma while words and cues remain intact; policy never travels in the payload. Existing per-view lookup tables are removed by their P9-P14 owners, and P16 freezes the final monopoly rather than this foundation claiming that migration early.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. The table and catalogue keys are additive; regenerated dashboards carry the vocabulary automatically."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-8",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the bundled dashboard demo now exercises every renderable vocabulary value and every bounded collection",
          "detail": "The demo now carries all 91 fixture-instantiable family/value pairs, including every one of the 25 lifecycle transitions, while the five browser-local transport states remain covered by their live-client scenarios. Work, consistency and document previews deliberately cross their server limits, so the visible sample proves shown, total and truncated facts instead of presenting a prefix as complete.\nFixture coverage is derived from the binary catalogue rather than a copied list. The committed compact dashboard data, indented CLI JSON and embedded template are regenerated projections of that same model, and the gate fails by the exact missing family/value when a future catalogue addition lacks a visible fixture carrier.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard",
            "a2a html --demo",
            "a2a html --json"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Regenerate a demo dashboard with the current binary to inspect the expanded state and bounds coverage."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-9",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the local dashboard now keeps one bilingual card, one status vocabulary and one explicit refresh path",
          "detail": "Overview, Exchange, Threads, Contracts, Map and Spaces now consume the server-carried sets, ordering, reasons, windows and byte bounds directly. Seven canonical card kinds render the same ordered RU/EN facts from every entry point; the map's parallel detail dialog is removed, unknown values never leak raw, and one resolver owns status words, cues and chroma. Release sequences preserve the carried server order; the remaining browser sorts are bounded, named presentation-only layout operations. Portable schemas now freeze the seven card object shapes, including the nested thread facts, while deliberately leaving the local dashboard envelope outside the public contract. The loopback server's CSP admits the bundled component evaluator while continuing to deny remote scripts, styles, images and non-self connections.\nLive SSE messages now announce a newer digest without replacing the screen. Refresh dashboard performs one conditional fetch and one atomic hydrate, preserving the selected view, filters, hash, focus, scroll and mounted card state; a removed card remains readable with an exact gone banner over retained last-good data. File and non-loopback dashboards remain inert. The external GitHub provider/API tier is deliberately deferred; deterministic Open on GitHub links still use only the carried repository and revision and perform no network request.\n",
          "affects": [
            "dashboard",
            "serve",
            "a2a html"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Regenerate or reopen the dashboard to use the coherent views and explicit refresh control."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-10",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a space's notification routes are declared, validated and refused when malformed",
          "detail": "`notification_routes` has shipped in the space template since it was written, and nothing validated it: the manifest schema does not declare the key, and `additionalProperties: true` accepted anything at all. A route with a typo in it was silence, discovered only by a notification that never arrived.\nThe shape is now enforced by the space's own pull-request gate. Every route must name a channel, a chat and at least one event; unknown keys, wrong types, values outside an enum, a duplicate event, a chat that is not a numeric string, and more than sixteen routes are each refused with the path that is wrong. A route whose `for` names a participant the manifest does not list, or that duplicates another route's target, is refused as well, and a manifest carrying anything shaped like a Telegram bot token is refused outright.\nValidation lives in the policy layer rather than in the schema, because `manifest/v1`'s bytes are frozen: they decide whether documents already committed to shared spaces are valid. The open schema accepts the key on every binary already deployed, so adopting this needs no upgrade, no floor bump and no workflow bump — only a route worth adding.\nThe agent-prompt tables and the bilingual dashboard vocabulary also moved out of the presentation package into their own, so a caller that is not a browser can quote the same words and the same legal moves. Nothing rendered changes.\n",
          "affects": [
            "space.yaml",
            "a2a validate",
            "dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. A space with no notification_routes, or with an empty list, is unaffected; the key is optional and defaults to none."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-11",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a notify` turns a push into chat messages, from the repository alone",
          "detail": "`a2a notify render` reads a space checkout and prints, as JSON, the messages a push would produce: one per configured route per qualifying artifact, carrying the type, who sent it to whom, whose move it is now, priority and deadline, the artifact's own text, and — for a route that names a participant — a copy-paste block that hands the move to an agent.\nIt needs no local cache. A CI runner has none, so the facts come from the repository itself, folded through the same machinery every other read surface uses rather than a second implementation that could disagree with it. `--only \u003cid,id\u003e` renders exactly the artifacts you name, however old or settled, which is how a layout gets reviewed on real documents.\n`a2a notify send` reads that array and delivers it. A failed send fails the command: a notification that silently did not fire is the same defect as a check that silently passed. A missing token is refused before anything is sent, naming the variable that is missing; Telegram's own rate limiting is honoured and then, if it persists, reported rather than swallowed.\nMessages render two ways over one set of facts — HTML by default, and Telegram's newer rich blocks per route — so a newer chat capability can never become a second version of what a message says. `--dry-run` prints exactly what would be sent without sending it.\nAnything shaped like a credential is stripped from an artifact's text before it can reach a chat, and the bot token cannot appear in any output. It is held in a type that prints as [redacted], and no error that touched a URL carrying it is ever wrapped or reformatted — both the network layer and the request builder embed the full address in their own error text, and Telegram's addresses carry the token, so those errors are replaced rather than passed along.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a notify"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. The verb is inert without a configured notification route; delivery from CI arrives with the workflow in a later change."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-12",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a space can be told to notify Telegram, in one line and one secret",
          "detail": "A space repository gains chat notifications by adding one `uses:` line and one Actions secret. It carries no notification logic, no pinned binary and no fetch token: everything lives in a reusable workflow in a2ahub, referenced by release tag, so a fix reaches every space through a version bump instead of an edit in each one. `a2a space init` writes the caller for new spaces; `a2a space update` adds it to existing ones.\nThe job is silent until a route is configured, and loud when one is broken: a route whose secret is missing fails the run rather than quietly sending nothing. What was sent — and, on a dry run, exactly what it would have looked like — is printed in the run's own summary, so a layout can be reviewed before it reaches a chat and a failure can be read without opening Telegram.\n`a2a notify setup` walks a human through creating the bot, handing over its token, choosing a chat, and proving the whole path works. The token is typed at the terminal with the screen echo off, passed straight to GitHub's secret store, and never written down anywhere else — no agent involved in the conversation ever sees it. The setup reports success only after a real run actually delivered a message; a green run that sent nothing is reported as unproven, because it is.\n`a2a doctor` gains three checks for a configured space: whether every route is well-formed, whether the secrets they name exist (presence only — no secret's value is ever read), and whether the last delivery succeeded. A channel that has gone quiet is now diagnosable instead of merely silent.\nThe MCP surface gains an `a2a_notify` tool with the same five actions, and it is registered but NOT wired: every call answers that notify operations are not available. It is listed here because an agent enumerating tools will find it and needs to know to use the CLI verbs instead — a tool that is present and always refuses, with nothing saying why, is worse than one that is absent.\n",
          "affects": [
            "space.yaml",
            "a2a space init",
            "a2a space update",
            "a2a notify",
            "a2a doctor",
            "MCP"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "Optional per space. Existing spaces gain nothing until an operator runs `a2a space update` and then `a2a notify setup`. A space that does neither is unchanged."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-13",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "every space validates and notifies on a Go toolchain with six fewer called vulnerabilities",
          "detail": "The toolchain moves from go1.26.5 to go1.26.6, which fixes all six standard-library vulnerabilities `govulncheck` reports as reachable from this code: quadratic path resolution in `net/url`, JavaScript regexp context tracking in `html/template`, post-handshake message flooding in `crypto/tls`, a missing `ReadHeaderTimeout` on the unencrypted HTTP/2 check in `net/http`, unbounded recursion in `encoding/asn1` (reached through detached cosign verification), and ASCII-only punycode labels in `x/net/idna`. The accepted-findings list stays empty: every called vulnerability is a hard red again.\nA space repository has no `go.mod`, so the two reusable workflows name the toolchain version explicitly while repo-local jobs read the file. That split had no reader, and bumping one without the other would have left every space on the vulnerable toolchain silently. `workflow-lint` now refuses any explicit `go-version:` that `go.mod` does not name.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a-validate-reusable.yml",
            "a2a-notify-reusable.yml"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "No action for an existing space pinned to an earlier release; a space adopts the new toolchain when its workflow pin moves to this release."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-14",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "publishing a second contract version is no longer blocked by the first publication's surviving branch",
          "detail": "The remote publication-head probe aborted its whole listing on the first error from any head, before it had asked whether that head was even relevant. So a first publish's merged-but-unpruned branch could refuse every later publish with a recompute conflict, and a space with branch pruning turned off, or merely slow, would hit it while a space with pruning on would not.\nThe probe now skips a head whose recorded target version already resolves in the space's authoritative published history — the same question the publisher already asks and trusts one step earlier. A head that is genuinely in conflict, and not already published, still fails the probe exactly as before, and a skipped head is never eligible for the exact-intent repair path.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Nothing about a published contract's content or its history changes; a publish that used to be refused now proceeds."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-15",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "`a2a doctor` names the two automations it cannot see, instead of staying silent about them",
          "detail": "A new `automation coverage` row names both automations the onboarding recommends — a harness session-start hook, and a scheduled poll workflow in the participant's own repository — states that doctor cannot observe either, says why, and tells you to check them by hand.\nIt never FAILs, and that is the design rather than a shortfall. The session-start hook lives in your agent harness's own configuration, which a2a does not read or write; the poll workflow lives in your repository, not the space, so it is outside every path doctor looks at. A FAIL would claim doctor had seen a broken state it structurally cannot see.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a doctor"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. The row is informational; if you have not set up either automation, `onboarding.md` §8.6 describes both."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-16",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a space's merge check now runs a signed release binary instead of compiling one",
          "detail": "The reusable validation workflow downloads the signed `a2a-linux-amd64` for the release its pin names, and verifies that binary's keyless Sigstore signature before placing it anywhere executable. The certificate identity and OIDC issuer are the same pair `a2a update` already verifies with, so both paths accept exactly the same signer.\nThe integrity property is unchanged in kind. What `go install` bought was not a checksum but a public, append-only log anyone can inspect after the fact; Sigstore's transparency log plus the release workflow's own identity is the same guarantee. Verifying a checksum file fetched from the same release would NOT have been — whoever could replace the binary could replace the checksums beside it.\nA space still needs no token: the assets are public and the proof is a public log entry. Nothing in a space changes until its workflow pin moves to this release or later.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a-validate-reusable.yml"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "No action for a space pinned to an earlier release; a space adopts this when its workflow pin moves. Spaces that mirror the workflow rather than calling it must adopt the cosign step themselves."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-17",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a contract-publication conflict now says whether it is a version boundary or a real disagreement",
          "detail": "A recovered publication head now records the version of the binary that minted it, and the refusal distinguishes three cases: the same binary recomputing its own head, which is the only one that may indicate tampering; a head recorded before this field existed; and a head minted by a different version, which names both versions so it is clear which upgrade crossed the boundary.\nAll three remain refusals — a head whose proof cannot be established is not a head to trust — but only one of them accuses anyone.\nHeads written by earlier releases keep working: the new field is optional on read, and its absence is reported as a compatibility state rather than folded into a conflict.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a contract publish"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Existing publication heads decode unchanged; a conflict message may now name two versions where it previously did not."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-18",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "picking a row in Exchange opens its detail beside the list again, instead of a dialog that repeats the row",
          "detail": "Exchange rows select into an inline detail pane, the way Contracts and Threads already did. The pane is in the page, not a dialog: it traps no focus, locks no scrolling and paints no backdrop, so reading a document no longer covers the list it came from.\nThe card dialog is unchanged and still opens from Overview and from a `#card=` link, which is what a saved link keeps doing. Its own frame no longer paints a focus ring around itself when it opens, and its padding is sized for card content rather than for a layout it no longer has.\nA selection now survives an in-place refresh together with the filter, the space and the scroll offset — and a selected row that disappears from the newer data closes the pane rather than leaving it describing something the data no longer carries.\nKnown limit, measured rather than assumed: selecting a row still returns the page to the top. That is not new here and not specific to Exchange — Contracts, which never lost its pane, does the same. It is the shared re-render dropping the scroll offset, and it is recorded for a fix in the shell rather than patched per screen.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Deep links, the card dialog and every existing route behave as before."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-19",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "dashboard screens stop printing placeholder sentences for facts the snapshot does not carry",
          "detail": "A fact that is not in the snapshot is now absent from the card rather than present as \"Not recorded.\" — an empty row taught nothing and cost a line of attention on every card. Facts that ARE carried read the same as before.\nOverview's process cards regained their grouping and their author lines, and the reader's own turn moved back out of the card into the list chrome, where it belongs: a card can be shared, and whose move it is depends on who is looking.\nExchange regained both facet axes — direction and open/closed — plus the type filter and the deadline chip, and its rows are readable at a glance again: badge, state, priority, deadline-or-age, title, and who is handing to whom. The detail that used to crowd the row now lives in the pane beside it.\nThreads, Spaces and the map were re-read against Contracts, the one screen that never regressed. Versions and the timeline card needed no change, which was measured rather than assumed.\nTwo counts on Exchange can disagree when a collection is truncated: the direction chips report the whole carried window, the new axes report what is in the current view. The sentence directly below the chips states the bound, and neither number is invented.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Every route, deep link and card door behaves as before."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-20",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the dashboard's card evidence now describes what the cards render",
          "detail": "Every card kind exposes its summary region, so a card's short form and its full form can be told apart and compared. Four kinds previously marked that region only while their own dialog was open, which never happened — a row is a card's short form whether or not anything is open, and it is now marked that way.\nThe remaining placeholder sentences are gone from every screen this release touched, and the assertions that had pinned them now assert the absence instead.\nA dialog that merely repeats the row it was opened from is now a test failure rather than a thing a reader has to notice.\nContracts is unchanged throughout, deliberately: it is the reference the other screens were re-read against, and its card content is byte-for-byte what it was.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. This release changes what the tests know, not what the dashboard does, beyond the copy fixes described above."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-21",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "Exchange opens on everything again, not silently on incoming only",
          "detail": "Opening Exchange showed only incoming documents while the screen's own state asked for all of them. Nothing said so: the \"All\" chip was gone, no other chip looked selected, and the shorter list read as \"this is what there is\". On a real project that was two rows where the previous release showed five.\n\"All\" is back as the first direction and is honoured again. Its count is what it can actually see, and it reports no window bound of its own — three separate bounds do not add up to one, and a total nobody sent is not a number worth printing.\nThe narrower reading of a value a screen does not recognise is the same defect class this release fixes elsewhere by refusing outright. Here the value was always legitimate; it was the screen that stopped understanding it.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a dashboard"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. If you had selected a direction, that selection still applies; only the default reading changed back."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-22",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a document's outcome now answers about THAT document, not about its (kind, state) pair",
          "detail": "`fold.OutcomeOf(kind, state)` is a pure function of two inputs, and for two pairs the true answer needs a third that only the envelope carries: an announcement's `ack_requested`, and whether a published contract has a registered consumer. `internal/pendency` already answered both correctly per document, so neither side was wrong — the seam between them was.\n`OutcomeOf` keeps its exact signature and answer. `OutcomeOfDocument` takes the caller-resolved fact, which is `LegalNextFor`'s own shipped shape for the identical question. The liveness half moved in the same change: `exchangeActive` short-circuited every kind but published announcements to \"active\", so a published contract nobody consumes stayed in the exchange feed forever.\nThe deprecation acknowledgement set is now resolved space-wide rather than from the local system's registry alone, so the author of a deprecation sees a late adopter who has not acknowledged.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. Nothing you author changes; a card that used to say a document was open with nobody waiting now says it is settled."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-23",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a response can finally say which acceptance criteria it did not satisfy",
          "detail": "`a2a respond` gains `--unmet`, `--standing` and `--blocked-by`, and fresh responses render `envelope/v2`. The MCP tool moves with the CLI — the two are independent readers, so a fix on one side is not a fix.\nA partial answer that names nothing is now refusable rather than the only shape available: the schema requires a `partial` or `cannot` to carry either the unmet set with what would unblock it, or a non-authoritative standing.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Use `--unmet`, `--standing` or `--blocked-by` on `a2a respond` when an answer falls short. A response that satisfies everything needs none of them."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-24",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a response's idempotency key now includes what the response says it failed",
          "detail": "The key folded in parents, result, fields, body and refs — everything the operation IS — and not the shortfall facts. Adding them widened the signature rather than adding a second entry point, deliberately: a key without these facts is not a narrower key, it is a colliding one, and leaving a callable narrow form would have left the defect reachable by accident.\nA caller that declares none of the three derives exactly the key it derived before, so no in-flight operation is disturbed.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action. A response that declares none of the three derives exactly the key it derived before, so no operation in flight is disturbed."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-25",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "an MCP write can no longer be silently prepared against the wrong space",
          "detail": "Server construction is per-space now, and a write that cannot name its target while several spaces are connected is REFUSED, naming the connected spaces — no default, because a default is how this defect gets recreated one layer up. A single-space session is unchanged.\nPartial, and stated as such: several write tools' inputs carry no field that could name a space at all, so a call cannot express the target even in principle. That is a tool-contract change and is not in this release.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "If your MCP session connects to more than one space, a write that cannot name its target is now refused rather than prepared against the first one. Name the space, or connect one."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-26",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "low",
          "subject": "every validation error code is now checked for prose, reachability and scope",
          "detail": "A new gate reads the error registry and refuses a code that appears in no shipped prose, that no test proves fires through a production entry point, or that rejects post-merge without a stated reason that it may judge artifacts predating it.\nAgainst the shipped corpus it found 26 gaps over 22 codes. They are declared in the registry with reasons and owners rather than closed silently, so the gate is green and BLOCKS the next code from joining them. Codes that predate the prose rule carry that as their stated reason, because applying a rule retroactively to history nobody may edit is the very thing the rule forbids.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "No action for a space. This gate runs in this repository and constrains what a future validation code may ship without."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-27",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a criterion can carry a name, and a verdict can use it",
          "detail": "`a2a verify` and `a2a close` now print each binding — the criterion's own first characters beside the verdict — before anything is minted. That alone would have caught the reported incident at composition.\nAcceptance criteria may also carry an id. Where a parent declares them, `--verdict ac3:met:seomatrix` names the criterion instead of counting to it, and an id that resolves to nothing is refused locally, naming what the parent does declare. The array is homogeneous: all ordinals or all ids, because a mixed array has no single addressing mode.\nArtifacts already written keep the ordinal form and stay valid. The older envelope generation is immutable history and is not touched.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Give acceptance criteria ids if you want verdicts to name them. Existing criteria keep working unchanged, addressed by position."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-28",
          "kind": "fix",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "closing an exchange now has to judge every criterion it was given",
          "detail": "A verify or close over a parent that declares criteria must name a verdict for each, or it is refused at the merge gate — with the missing ones named. The enum already carries `not_warranted` and `not_exercised`, so a criterion that did not apply still gets a verdict and nobody is forced to claim something false.\nExchanges already closed stay closed and stay green: the rule refuses at the write gate and returns no verdict against merged history, which no verb can rewrite.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "A close over a parent that declares acceptance criteria must now carry a verdict for each. Use `not_warranted` or `not_exercised` for one that did not apply. Exchanges already closed are unaffected."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-29",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "a handoff whose required sections say nothing is refused",
          "detail": "The five sections the domain requires must carry content. The check measures emptiness, never quality — there is no length floor, because a floor turns an empty handoff into a padded one, which reads as answered.\nThe section list comes from the handoff template itself, so a section added there is enforced the day it lands. Refused at the merge gate, silent against handoffs already merged.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "space",
            "why": "Fill the five sections of a handoff before submitting it. Handoffs already merged are unaffected."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-30",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "the Contracts screen says whether a contract's operational half exists",
          "detail": "A contract card now states one of three things: the operational half is ready, it is declared absent (and `a2a contract activate` is what clears it), or it was never declared at all. Those are three different facts and the screen no longer collapses them into one.\nA new warning fires when a contract invites a runtime pin against an operational half it declares absent. A warning rather than a refusal: publishing a promise before activating it is a designed sequence, so the pair is a claim ahead of the facts, not an invalid document.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Declare `x_operational` on a contract whose endpoint is not up yet, and run `a2a contract activate` when it is. A contract that declares nothing reads as \"not declared\" rather than as ready."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "RN-02300-31",
          "kind": "feat",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "doctor names a contract you consume but never adopted",
          "detail": "`a2a doctor` reports a contract this system has verify-passed conforming deliveries against while its own registry does not list it, with the delivery count and the exact `a2a contract adopt` that fixes it.\nDetection only, and a warning: consuming without adopting is a real risk rather than an error, and the operator may have reasons. Whether such a delivery should REGISTER a consumer is a separate question and is not answered here — the retire precondition is unchanged.\n",
          "action": {
            "scope": "local",
            "why": "Run `a2a doctor`. If it names a contract you consume but never adopted, run `a2a contract adopt \u003cid\u003e` — that is what puts you on the deprecation notice and behind the retire gate."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "KI-01900-PROVIDER-TIER-DEFERRED",
          "kind": "known-issue",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The current releases shipped with their provider-tier verification deferred, by explicit operator authorization",
          "detail": "Every release from v0.19.0 through v0.22.0 shipped this way — FIFTEEN of them, each authorized separately for its own candidate, and the count is machine-refused unless the newest record signs for its own position in the streak.\nThe number was TEN until 2026-08-13, and the correction is worth stating plainly rather than quietly applying: the gate counted deferral RECORDS, not releases, so a release that simply omitted the file was invisible to the mechanism built to make the streak refusable — and omitting it was easier than writing it. Re-keying the gate to releases found three that had shipped with no record at all (v0.19.4, v0.19.5, v0.19.11) where one was known. Each now carries a retrospective record stating what it is: a record of an ABSENCE, carrying no signature, asserting only that no decision was written down — never that the tier did not run, which is exactly the state no evidence points either way about. Their scenario LOGIC is fully verified: the whole catalogue runs locally against a faithful host stand-in over real git repositories, and every row that tier may judge passed on the exact published candidate. What neither carries is a full live GitHub matrix. The rows therefore unverified are the ones only a real provider can decide: branch protection blocking a merge until the required check is green, enforce_admins binding a participant while skipping an admin, a cross-section authorization decision surviving a privileged re-trigger, GitHub serving a check computed against a stale ref, a direct push to main being refused, the shipped Actions workflow actually running `a2a validate --ci`, compound check-run name resolution, a fine-grained token's scope non-reporting driving the preflight path, real concurrent writes losing nothing, and the space's own CI carrying no unexplained failures. The last full matrix that did prove them ran on 2026-08-05, against a tree earlier than all seven of these releases. Read the cost concretely rather than as a formality. v0.19.0 changed the contract publication path; v0.19.1 changed the git authentication used by every remote call in the write funnel; v0.19.2 and v0.19.3 changed how credentials are resolved for reads; v0.19.6 changed `a2a validate --ci` itself and the contract publication planner. In every case the deferred rows sit over the surfaces that changed — which the runbook names as the worst case for this tier, and it was chosen each time with that visible. v0.19.6 is the sharpest instance so far: \"the shipped Actions workflow actually running `a2a validate --ci`\" is one of the unproven rows, and that command is what this release changed. v0.19.9 is the widest: it changes how the ACTOR is resolved on every write verb on both the CLI and the MCP surface, so what is unproven is the identity recorded by writes going through a real provider. v0.19.10 is the widest of the nine by a wide margin: it is the whole agent-exchange epic, and the surfaces it changes — the write funnel, the validator CI runs, the shipped reusable workflow — are exactly the ones the unproven rows cover. The delivery-and-verdict loop this release is built around has never been driven through a real pull request; it is proven against a faithful in-process GitHub double and real local git repositories, and never against GitHub itself. A provider-tier run is scheduled against the newest of these tags, and anything it finds ships as a patch release. Nine consecutive deferrals over the same surfaces is not improving with repetition, and the eighth passed a date the seventh record had itself named as the run's deadline; the signed count in the newest deferral record is the honest measure of how long this has been outstanding.\n",
          "affects": [
            "a2a submit",
            "a2a contract publish",
            "space mirror",
            "branch protection",
            "space CI"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Nothing to do. Report anything that misbehaves through `a2a feedback` — that channel is what turns a deferred verification into a finding rather than a silence.\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "KI-MACOS-ADHOC-SIGNING",
          "kind": "known-issue",
          "impact": "normal",
          "subject": "The macOS companion remains ad-hoc signed and may require manual approval",
          "detail": "The release cohort authenticates the exact macOS app and ad-hoc signing protects its bundle integrity, but the app is not identified or notarized through a paid Apple Developer account. A quarantined download may require one explicit System Settings → Privacy \u0026 Security → Open Anyway decision. Interactive Notification Center, Focus, Login Items, VS Code trust/profile, and remote-window checks remain release-environment evidence rather than claims inferred from hermetic tests.\n",
          "affects": [
            "macOS notifications",
            "VS Code notifications"
          ],
          "action": {
            "scope": "none",
            "why": "Keep Gatekeeper enabled and approve only the A2A Notifier version obtained through the verified a2a release cohort.\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "updateDetail": {
    "status": "verified",
    "version": "0.16.3",
    "headline": "Statusline refresh now survives the one-shot command, while the merge gate becomes deterministic and cache-aware",
    "changes": [
      {
        "kind": "fix",
        "impact": "normal",
        "subject": "A stale statusline launches one durable refresh for the next render",
        "detail": "The prompt-facing statusline still reads local cache only and keeps its existing text, JSON, and exit-code contract. When that cache is stale, it now atomically claims a short-lived local lease and starts the canonical `a2a sync` command as a detached process. The refresh can finish after the one-shot statusline process exits, concurrent prompt renders do not start a herd of duplicate syncs even while recovering an expired lease, and the next render sees the refreshed mirror. The previous in-process goroutine could be terminated by normal CLI exit or race a test fixture's cleanup.",
        "run": [
          "a2a update --yes"
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "fix",
        "impact": "low",
        "subject": "The ordinary CI gate no longer depends on ambient Git identity or a cold duplicate Go cache",
        "detail": "Git-backed tests now receive a process-local fixture identity, the checkout-owned validation runner has a documented scoped-test entrypoint, GitHub's setup-go cache feeds the exact build cache consumed by `make check`, and workflow syntax validation fails closed with pinned actionlint. The public-only classification backstop no longer rejects the private source repository, and CodeQL has the read scope its analyze step needs under restrictive workflow permissions. CodeQL execution is scoped to the public repository where code scanning is enabled; private CI keeps its gosec, govulncheck, and gitleaks gates without spending minutes on an upload GitHub will refuse. Lifecycle wire parity retains every case while reusing one immutable repository fixture."
      },
      {
        "kind": "fix",
        "impact": "normal",
        "subject": "Standing known issues no longer disappear on the next update",
        "detail": "Current limitations now live once in a schema-validated standing list instead of being copied into every version file. `a2a whatsnew`, its MCP twin, the HTML release view, and the authored GitHub Release body append that list independently of the selected version range, including when there are no newer change entries. The existing release-array machine shape remains unchanged, and a resolved issue is removed in one place."
      },
      {
        "kind": "policy",
        "impact": "low",
        "subject": "The v0.16.3 runtime candidate passed all 50 declared live cells",
        "detail": "The immutable public runtime candidate d6418b926ec5363416ef8b42a572788e7e7e009a completed the full protected GitHub matrix with two independent identities: 50 pass, zero fail, zero timed-out, and zero not-run cells across CLI, MCP, lifecycle, contracts, authorization boundaries, failure recovery, thread reconstruction, and space migration. The audit observed 179 workflow runs; its two red runs were the expected and explicitly claimed cross-section boundary probes. The final tagged tree differs from that runtime candidate only by this evidence statement and the matching README baseline; local gates, release preflight, renderer checks, and the filtered-public leak proof are repeated on the exact tag candidate."
      }
    ]
  },
